B.VR=s(["Document it as an expected finding for this age","Ask unlicensed assistive personnel to recheck it tomorrow","No action is needed if the client is sleeping"],t.s)
B.a0C=s(["Beta blockers (-olol) lower heart rate, blood pressure, and myocardial oxygen demand.","Hold a beta blocker and notify the provider if heart rate is below 60/min or systolic BP is below 90 mm Hg.","Never stop a beta blocker abruptly; tapering prevents rebound tachycardia and angina.","Beta blockers can mask tachycardia, the early warning sign of hypoglycemia, in diabetic clients.","Nonselective beta blockers such as propranolol can trigger bronchospasm; avoid in asthma.","Calcium channel blockers (verapamil, diltiazem, amlodipine) relax vessels and can cause dizziness, headache, and peripheral edema.","Teach clients on calcium channel blockers to avoid grapefruit juice, which raises drug levels."],t.s)
B.adA=new A.aw("Beta Blockers & Calcium Channel Blockers",B.a0C)
B.UN=s(["ACE inhibitors end in -pril (lisinopril, enalapril); ARBs end in -sartan (losartan, valsartan).","A dry, persistent cough is a classic ACE inhibitor effect; switching to an ARB usually resolves it.","Both classes raise potassium; monitor for hyperkalemia and avoid salt substitutes containing potassium.","Angioedema (swelling of lips, tongue, face) is a life-threatening reaction; stop the drug and treat emergently.","Both are contraindicated in pregnancy because of fetal harm.","Give the first dose at bedtime and rise slowly to reduce first-dose orthostatic hypotension."],t.s)
B.aeO=new A.aw("ACE Inhibitors & ARBs",B.UN)
B.Y1=s(["Digoxin slows heart rate and strengthens contraction; therapeutic level is roughly 0.5 to 2 ng/mL.","Count the apical pulse for one full minute; hold the dose and call the provider if it is below 60/min in an adult.","Early toxicity signs: anorexia, nausea, vomiting; later signs include visual changes such as yellow-green halos.","Hypokalemia dramatically increases the risk of digoxin toxicity, so monitor potassium closely with loop diuretics.","Digoxin immune Fab is the antidote for serious toxicity.","In infants, a heart rate below about 90 to 110/min (per facility policy) is the hold criterion."],t.s)
B.adM=new A.aw("Digoxin",B.Y1)
B.Uf=s(["For acute angina: rest, then one sublingual nitroglycerin tablet every 5 minutes, up to three doses.","Call emergency services if chest pain is unrelieved after the first tablet (per current guidelines) or persists.","Expect a headache and possible flushing; these reflect vasodilation and usually fade with continued use.","Store tablets in the original dark glass container, away from heat and light, and replace about every 6 months.","Remove transdermal nitroglycerin patches for 10 to 12 hours daily (often overnight) to prevent tolerance.","Nitrates plus erectile dysfunction drugs (sildenafil and similar) cause severe hypotension; the combination is contraindicated."],t.s)
B.aes=new A.aw("Nitrates",B.Uf)
B.Z5=s(["Loop diuretics (furosemide) waste potassium; watch for muscle cramps, weakness, and dysrhythmias.","Give IV furosemide slowly; rapid push risks ototoxicity (tinnitus, hearing loss).","Thiazides (hydrochlorothiazide) also lower potassium and can raise glucose, calcium, and uric acid.","Spironolactone spares potassium; teach clients to avoid potassium-rich salt substitutes and watch for hyperkalemia.","Schedule diuretics in the morning so nighttime urination does not disturb sleep or increase fall risk.","Weigh daily; a gain of 1 kg (2.2 lb) overnight equals about 1 liter of retained fluid."],t.s)
B.adh=new A.aw("Diuretics",B.Z5)
B.Wn=s([B.adA,B.aeO,B.adM,B.aes,B.adh],t.u)
B.af7=new A.di("pharm","Cardiac Medications",B.Wn)
B.YS=s(["IV heparin is monitored with aPTT; therapeutic range is roughly 1.5 to 2 times the control value.","Protamine sulfate is the antidote for heparin overdose.","A platelet drop of more than half from baseline suggests heparin-induced thrombocytopenia; stop heparin and notify the provider.","Give subcutaneous heparin in the abdomen, do not aspirate, and do not massage the site.","Heparin does not dissolve existing clots; it prevents new clot formation and extension."],t.s)
B.aeJ=new A.aw("Heparin",B.YS)
B.a_B=s(["Warfarin is monitored with INR; the usual therapeutic target is 2 to 3 (higher for mechanical valves).","Vitamin K is the antidote for warfarin overdose.","Keep vitamin K intake (leafy greens) consistent rather than eliminating it; sudden changes swing the INR.","Warfarin takes 3 to 5 days to reach full effect, so heparin overlap (bridging) is common at the start.","Warfarin is teratogenic and contraindicated in pregnancy.","Many drugs interact with warfarin; tell clients to check before starting any new medication, including OTC and herbal products."],t.s)
B.adC=new A.aw("Warfarin",B.a_B)
B.VP=s(["Enoxaparin (LMWH) is dosed subcutaneously and usually needs no routine lab monitoring.","Do not expel the air bubble in a prefilled enoxaparin syringe; it seals the dose in the tissue.","Direct oral anticoagulants (apixaban, rivaroxaban, dabigatran) need no routine INR checks.","Idarucizumab reverses dabigatran; andexanet alfa reverses apixaban and rivaroxaban.","Renal function guides dosing for LMWH and DOACs; impaired kidneys raise bleeding risk."],t.s)
B.adZ=new A.aw("LMWH & Direct Oral Anticoagulants",B.VP)
B.Z0=s(["Aspirin and clopidogrel prevent platelet clumping; both are held before many surgeries per provider order.","Teach all anticoagulated clients: soft toothbrush, electric razor, no contact sports, and no NSAIDs without approval.","Report black tarry stools, coffee-ground emesis, pink or dark urine, or bleeding that will not stop.","Apply firm pressure to puncture sites for longer than usual and avoid intramuscular injections when possible.","Clients should carry medical alert identification stating they take an anticoagulant."],t.s)
B.aea=new A.aw("Antiplatelets & Bleeding Precautions",B.Z0)
B.YH=s([B.aeJ,B.adC,B.adZ,B.aea],t.u)
B.aff=new A.di("pharm","Anticoagulants & Antiplatelets",B.YH)
B.Zg=s(["Rapid-acting (lispro, aspart): onset 10 to 30 minutes, peak 30 minutes to 3 hours, lasts 3 to 5 hours; give with food in sight.","Short-acting regular insulin: onset 30 to 60 minutes, peak 2 to 5 hours, lasts 5 to 8 hours; the only insulin given IV.","Intermediate NPH: onset 1 to 2 hours, peak 4 to 12 hours, lasts 14 to 24 hours; cloudy appearance is normal.","Long-acting (glargine, detemir): onset about 1 hour, no pronounced peak, lasts up to 24 hours.","Never mix glargine or detemir with any other insulin in the same syringe.","Hypoglycemia risk is highest at each insulin's peak time."],t.s)
B.adH=new A.aw("Insulin Types: Onset, Peak, Duration",B.Zg)
B.Zb=s(["When mixing, draw clear (regular) before cloudy (NPH) to avoid contaminating the regular vial.","Roll NPH gently between the palms to mix; do not shake.","Rotate sites within one anatomic region to prevent lipohypertrophy; the abdomen absorbs fastest.","Unopened insulin is refrigerated; the vial in use may stay at room temperature for about 28 days.","During illness, clients should keep taking insulin, check glucose more often, and test urine ketones."],t.s)
B.aez=new A.aw("Insulin Administration & Mixing",B.Zb)
B.a11=s(["Signs: shakiness, sweating, tachycardia, hunger, irritability, confusion; onset is sudden.","Treat a conscious client with 15 g of fast carbohydrate: 4 oz juice or regular soda, or glucose tablets.","Recheck glucose in 15 minutes and repeat treatment if still below 70 mg/dL (the 15/15 rule).","Once glucose recovers, follow with a snack of protein plus complex carbohydrate if the next meal is over an hour away.","For an unconscious client, give IM/subcut glucagon or IV dextrose; never put anything in the mouth."],t.s)
B.adL=new A.aw("Hypoglycemia Recognition & Treatment",B.a11)
B.a02=s(["Metformin rarely causes hypoglycemia alone but can cause GI upset and, rarely, lactic acidosis.","Hold metformin the day of and 48 hours after IV contrast dye; recheck renal function before restarting.","Sulfonylureas (glipizide, glyburide) stimulate insulin release and can cause true hypoglycemia; avoid alcohol.","GLP-1 agonists (semaglutide, liraglutide) slow gastric emptying; nausea is common and pancreatitis is a red flag.","SGLT2 inhibitors (-flozin) increase glucose in urine, raising risk of genital and urinary infections and dehydration."],t.s)
B.aex=new A.aw("Oral Agents & Non-Insulin Injectables",B.a02)
B.W3=s([B.adH,B.aez,B.adL,B.aex],t.u)
B.afb=new A.di("pharm","Diabetes Medications",B.W3)
B.Ut=s(["SSRIs (sertraline, fluoxetine, escitalopram) take 2 to 4 weeks or more for full antidepressant effect.","Monitor closely for increased suicide risk as energy returns before mood lifts, especially in young adults.","Serotonin syndrome: agitation, fever, sweating, tremor, hyperreflexia; risk rises when combined with other serotonergic drugs or St. John's wort.","Common effects include GI upset, insomnia, and sexual dysfunction; take with food to reduce nausea.","Do not stop SSRIs abruptly; taper to avoid discontinuation symptoms such as dizziness and irritability."],t.s)
B.aeo=new A.aw("SSRIs & SNRIs",B.Ut)
B.a_X=s(["MAOIs (phenelzine, tranylcypromine) plus tyramine-rich food can trigger hypertensive crisis.","Avoid aged cheeses, cured and smoked meats, fermented foods, soy sauce, sauerkraut, draft beer, and red wine.","Severe occipital headache, stiff neck, palpitations, and sharply elevated BP signal hypertensive crisis; treat emergently.","Allow a washout of about 2 weeks between an MAOI and most other antidepressants (5 weeks after fluoxetine).","Many OTC cold and decongestant products interact with MAOIs; clients must check before use."],t.s)
B.adc=new A.aw("MAOIs & Tyramine",B.a_X)
B.Ye=s(["Maintenance lithium level is roughly 0.6 to 1.2 mEq/L; toxicity risk climbs above 1.5 mEq/L.","Early toxicity: nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, fine tremor; worsening brings coarse tremor, ataxia, confusion, seizures.","Dehydration, low sodium intake, NSAIDs, and diuretics all raise lithium levels.","Keep salt and fluid intake steady (2 to 3 L water daily); do not start a low-sodium diet without provider input.","Draw trough levels about 12 hours after the last dose; monitor thyroid and renal function long term."],t.s)
B.ade=new A.aw("Lithium",B.Ye)
B.ZA=s(["First-generation agents (haloperidol) carry high risk of extrapyramidal symptoms (EPS).","Acute dystonia (muscle spasms of neck, jaw, eyes) is treated urgently with benztropine or diphenhydramine.","Akathisia is uncontrollable restlessness; do not mistake it for anxiety or agitation.","Tardive dyskinesia (lip smacking, tongue writhing) may be irreversible; report earliest signs.","Neuroleptic malignant syndrome: high fever, board-like rigidity, autonomic instability, altered consciousness; stop the drug and treat as an emergency.","Second-generation agents cause metabolic effects; monitor weight, glucose, and lipids.","Clozapine requires scheduled ANC monitoring because of agranulocytosis risk; report fever or sore throat at once."],t.s)
B.aep=new A.aw("Antipsychotics: EPS & NMS",B.ZA)
B.Zv=s(["Benzodiazepines (lorazepam, diazepam, alprazolam) are for short-term use because of dependence risk.","Combining benzodiazepines with alcohol or opioids can cause fatal respiratory depression.","Flumazenil reverses benzodiazepine overdose.","Abrupt discontinuation after regular use can precipitate seizures; always taper.","Institute fall precautions in older adults; sedation and ataxia are common."],t.s)
B.aem=new A.aw("Benzodiazepines",B.Zv)
B.WO=s([B.aeo,B.adc,B.ade,B.aep,B.aem],t.u)
B.af_=new A.di("pharm","Psychiatric Medications",B.WO)
B.UR=s(["Always ask about prior penicillin allergy before the first dose; anaphylaxis can occur within minutes.","A small percentage of penicillin-allergic clients cross-react with cephalosporins; verify orders when allergy history exists.","Take the full prescribed course even after symptoms resolve to prevent resistance.","Watch for superinfection signs: white oral patches, vaginal itching, or new watery diarrhea.","Ceftriaxone with concurrent alcohol can produce a disulfiram-like reaction with some cephalosporins; advise avoiding alcohol."],t.s)
B.aei=new A.aw("Penicillins & Cephalosporins",B.UR)
B.UD=s(["Aminoglycosides (gentamicin, tobramycin) are nephrotoxic and ototoxic; monitor creatinine and ask about tinnitus.","Peak and trough levels guide aminoglycoside and vancomycin dosing; draw the trough just before the next dose.","Infuse vancomycin over at least 60 minutes; rapid infusion causes flushing and hypotension (infusion reaction).","Report ringing in the ears, hearing changes, or decreased urine output immediately.","Baseline and ongoing renal function tests are required for both drug groups."],t.s)
B.ae4=new A.aw("Aminoglycosides & Vancomycin",B.UD)
B.W9=s(["Fluoroquinolones (-floxacin) can cause tendinitis and tendon rupture; stop the drug and report tendon pain.","Fluoroquinolones and tetracyclines increase photosensitivity; use sunscreen and protective clothing.","Take tetracyclines on an empty stomach and separate from dairy, antacids, and iron, which block absorption.","Tetracyclines are avoided in pregnancy and in children under 8 because of tooth discoloration.","Macrolides (erythromycin, azithromycin) can prolong the QT interval; use caution with other QT-prolonging drugs."],t.s)
B.adQ=new A.aw("Fluoroquinolones, Tetracyclines & Macrolides",B.W9)
B.Y2=s(["Broad-spectrum antibiotics (clindamycin, fluoroquinolones, cephalosporins) carry the highest C. difficile risk.","New foul-smelling watery diarrhea during or after antibiotics warrants a stool test and contact precautions.","Wash hands with soap and water for C. diff; alcohol rub does not kill the spores.","Antibiotics do not treat viral illness; teach clients not to save or share leftover doses.","Some antibiotics reduce oral contraceptive reliability; advise a backup method during therapy."],t.s)
B.aeG=new A.aw("C. diff Risk & Client Teaching",B.Y2)
B.a_l=s(["Acyclovir treats herpes viruses; hydrate well because crystals can injure the kidneys, especially with IV dosing.","Oseltamivir works best when started within 48 hours of influenza symptom onset.","Antiretroviral therapy for HIV must be taken consistently every day; missed doses breed resistance.","Herpes antivirals suppress outbreaks but do not cure infection; transmission is still possible.","Teach clients on ganciclovir to report easy bruising or infection signs; it suppresses bone marrow."],t.s)
B.adt=new A.aw("Antivirals",B.a_l)
B.a1f=s([B.aei,B.ae4,B.adQ,B.aeG,B.adt],t.u)
B.af3=new A.di("pharm","Antibiotics & Antivirals",B.a1f)
B.VB=s(["Left-sided failure backs up into the lungs: crackles, dyspnea, orthopnea, cough with frothy pink sputum.","Right-sided failure backs up into the body: jugular vein distention, peripheral edema, hepatomegaly, ascites, weight gain.","Daily weight is the most reliable indicator of fluid status; report a gain of 1 kg in a day or 2.3 kg in a week.","BNP rises with heart failure severity and helps distinguish cardiac from pulmonary dyspnea.","Position in high Fowler with legs dependent during acute pulmonary edema; give oxygen and IV diuretics as ordered.","Teach a low-sodium diet, daily weights, and to report new orthopnea or needing extra pillows to sleep."],t.s)
B.aeb=new A.aw("Heart Failure: Left vs Right",B.VB)
B.YA=s(["Classic MI pain is crushing substernal pressure radiating to the left arm or jaw, unrelieved by rest or nitroglycerin.","Women, older adults, and diabetics often present atypically: fatigue, dyspnea, nausea, or epigastric discomfort.","Troponin is the most specific cardiac marker; it rises within hours and stays elevated for days.","Immediate care: 12-lead ECG within 10 minutes, oxygen if hypoxic, aspirin to chew, nitroglycerin, and pain control.","For STEMI, door-to-balloon (PCI) goal is 90 minutes; reperfusion speed determines muscle saved.","After MI, monitor for dysrhythmias, the most common early complication and leading cause of death before hospital arrival."],t.s)
B.adX=new A.aw("Myocardial Infarction",B.YA)
B.Xe=s(["Stable angina is predictable, triggered by exertion, and relieved by rest or nitroglycerin within minutes.","Unstable angina occurs at rest, is new or worsening, and is an emergency because infarction may be imminent.","Variant (Prinzmetal) angina results from coronary spasm, often at rest or at night, and responds to calcium channel blockers.","Troponin stays normal in angina; myocardial cells are ischemic but not yet necrotic.","Teach clients to stop activity, sit down, and use sublingual nitroglycerin at the first sign of chest pain."],t.s)
B.aej=new A.aw("Angina",B.Xe)
B.YO=s(["Hypertension is usually symptomless, which is why it is called the silent killer; damage accrues in heart, brain, kidneys, and eyes.","Confirm elevated readings on separate occasions with proper cuff size and the arm supported at heart level.","Lifestyle first line: sodium restriction, DASH-style diet, weight loss, regular exercise, limited alcohol, no smoking.","Teach clients never to stop antihypertensives abruptly; rebound hypertension can be severe.","Hypertensive crisis with organ symptoms (headache, vision change, chest pain) requires ICU titration of IV agents; lower BP gradually."],t.s)
B.adl=new A.aw("Hypertension",B.YO)
B.Xq=s([B.aeb,B.adX,B.aej,B.adl],t.u)
B.afi=new A.di("medsurg","Cardiovascular Disorders",B.Xq)
B.XU=s(["COPD clients often retain CO2; their baseline oxygen saturation target is typically 88 to 92 percent.","Give the lowest effective oxygen flow; watch for decreased respiratory drive with high-flow oxygen in chronic retainers.","Pursed-lip breathing prolongs exhalation and keeps airways open; teach it for dyspnea episodes.","Tripod positioning (leaning forward on a table) eases the work of breathing.","Barrel chest, clubbing, and prolonged expiration are classic chronic findings.","Small frequent high-calorie meals help; eating is exhausting and clients are often underweight.","Smoking cessation is the single most effective step to slow disease progression."],t.s)
B.aet=new A.aw("COPD",B.XU)
B.a_J=s(["Expiratory wheezing, chest tightness, cough, and dyspnea define an attack; triggers include allergens, exercise, and cold air.","A silent chest in a client who was wheezing signals impending respiratory failure, not improvement.","Short-acting beta agonists (albuterol) are rescue drugs; inhaled corticosteroids are daily controllers, not rescue.","Use the bronchodilator first when both inhalers are due, then the corticosteroid a few minutes later.","Rinse the mouth after inhaled corticosteroids to prevent oral thrush.","A falling peak flow (yellow or red zone of the personal plan) means worsening control; follow the action plan."],t.s)
B.adv=new A.aw("Asthma",B.a_J)
B.WI=s(["Typical findings: fever, productive cough, pleuritic chest pain, crackles, and dullness over consolidation.","Older adults may show only new confusion, weakness, or falls instead of fever and cough.","Obtain ordered sputum cultures before the first antibiotic dose whenever feasible.","Encourage 2 to 3 L of fluid daily (unless restricted), incentive spirometry, and early ambulation.","Aspiration risk rises with dysphagia, sedation, and tube feeding; keep the head of bed at 30 degrees or more.","Pneumococcal and influenza vaccines are key prevention teaching for high-risk groups."],t.s)
B.adD=new A.aw("Pneumonia",B.WI)
B.ZI=s(["Hallmarks: night sweats, low-grade fever, weight loss, fatigue, and cough lasting more than 3 weeks, possibly with blood.","Hospitalized clients with active TB need airborne precautions in a negative-pressure room; staff wear an N95 respirator.","Sputum acid-fast bacillus testing confirms active disease; a positive skin or blood test alone shows exposure, not active TB.","Standard therapy combines multiple drugs (such as rifampin, isoniazid, pyrazinamide, ethambutol) for 6 months or more.","Rifampin turns urine, sweat, and tears orange and reduces oral contraceptive effectiveness.","Isoniazid can cause peripheral neuropathy; vitamin B6 (pyridoxine) is given to prevent it.","Clients are usually noninfectious after about 2 to 3 weeks of effective therapy with improving symptoms and negative smears."],t.s)
B.ae7=new A.aw("Tuberculosis",B.ZI)
B.Wb=s([B.aet,B.adv,B.adD,B.ae7],t.u)
B.aeY=new A.di("medsurg","Respiratory Disorders",B.Wb)
B.Xs=s(["DKA: usually type 1, glucose often 300 to 600 mg/dL, ketones present, metabolic acidosis, Kussmaul respirations, fruity breath.","HHS: usually type 2, glucose often above 600 mg/dL, minimal ketones, profound dehydration, gradual onset with neuro changes.","First intervention for both is isotonic IV fluid; insulin infusion follows.","Insulin drives potassium into cells, so serum potassium falls during treatment; verify adequate potassium before and during the infusion.","Add dextrose to IV fluids once glucose falls to around 250 mg/dL to prevent hypoglycemia and cerebral edema.","Common DKA triggers: infection, missed insulin, and new-onset diabetes."],t.s)
B.adB=new A.aw("DKA vs HHS",B.Xs)
B.YZ=s(["Hypothyroidism: fatigue, weight gain, cold intolerance, constipation, dry skin, bradycardia, depression.","Take levothyroxine on an empty stomach each morning, 30 to 60 minutes before food, at the same time daily.","Levothyroxine therapy is lifelong; report palpitations or tremor, which suggest over-replacement.","Hyperthyroidism (Graves): weight loss with increased appetite, heat intolerance, tachycardia, tremor, exophthalmos.","Thyroid storm is an emergency: extreme fever, tachycardia, agitation, delirium; often triggered by stress, surgery, or infection.","After thyroidectomy keep a tracheostomy set, calcium gluconate, and suction at the bedside; watch for stridor and hypocalcemic tingling.","Post-thyroidectomy hoarseness may indicate laryngeal nerve injury; check calcium with Chvostek and Trousseau signs."],t.s)
B.aeL=new A.aw("Hypothyroidism & Hyperthyroidism",B.YZ)
B.YM=s(["Addison disease (low cortisol/aldosterone): weakness, weight loss, bronze skin, hypotension, hyponatremia, hyperkalemia, hypoglycemia.","Addisonian crisis brings severe hypotension and shock; treat with IV fluids, IV hydrocortisone, and glucose.","Clients on chronic steroids must never stop abruptly and need extra doses during illness, surgery, or major stress.","Cushing syndrome (excess cortisol): moon face, truncal obesity, buffalo hump, thin fragile skin, striae, hyperglycemia, hypertension.","Cushing clients are infection-prone with masked inflammation; protect skin and monitor glucose.","Teach steroid-dependent clients to wear medical alert identification."],t.s)
B.aeM=new A.aw("Adrenal Disorders",B.YM)
B.WT=s(["SIADH retains water: concentrated scant urine, dilute blood, hyponatremia, weight gain without edema.","SIADH care: fluid restriction, hypertonic saline for severe hyponatremia, and seizure precautions.","Diabetes insipidus loses water: liters of dilute urine, intense thirst, rising serum sodium and osmolality.","DI care: replace fluids and give desmopressin; weigh daily and track intake and output strictly.","Correct sodium slowly in both conditions; rapid shifts cause neurologic injury."],t.s)
B.ae2=new A.aw("Pituitary: SIADH & Diabetes Insipidus",B.WT)
B.X4=s([B.adB,B.aeL,B.aeM,B.ae2],t.u)
B.afk=new A.di("medsurg","Endocrine Disorders",B.X4)
B.a1n=s(["Gastric ulcer pain often worsens with eating; duodenal ulcer pain occurs 2 to 3 hours after meals and at night, relieved by food.","H. pylori infection and NSAID use are the two leading causes; treatment combines antibiotics with a proton pump inhibitor.","Sudden severe abdominal pain with a rigid, board-like abdomen suggests perforation; keep NPO and notify the provider immediately.","Avoid NSAIDs, aspirin, alcohol, smoking, and caffeine, which impair healing.","Melena or coffee-ground emesis indicates bleeding; check orthostatic vitals and hemoglobin."],t.s)
B.ae0=new A.aw("Peptic Ulcer Disease",B.a1n)
B.Wm=s(["Upper GI bleeding presents as hematemesis or melena; bright red rectal blood usually means a lower source.","First priorities: two large-bore IVs, isotonic fluids, type and crossmatch, and frequent vital signs.","Tachycardia and narrowing pulse pressure appear before blood pressure falls; do not wait for hypotension.","Keep the client NPO for probable endoscopy.","Monitor hemoglobin and hematocrit serially; the first values can look normal before hemodilution."],t.s)
B.adp=new A.aw("GI Bleed",B.Wm)
B.Wr=s(["Key findings: jaundice, ascites, spider angiomas, palmar erythema, easy bruising, and gynecomastia.","Esophageal varices can rupture; avoid straining, vigorous coughing, alcohol, and rough or hard foods.","Hepatic encephalopathy shows as confusion and asterixis (flapping tremor); rising ammonia is the culprit.","Lactulose lowers ammonia by trapping it in the gut; titrate to 2 to 3 soft stools daily.","Measure abdominal girth and weight daily for ascites; paracentesis relieves severe distention.","Low sodium intake and diuretics (spironolactone with furosemide) manage fluid; protein is moderated, not eliminated.","Clotting factors fall with liver failure; institute bleeding precautions."],t.s)
B.adq=new A.aw("Cirrhosis & Complications",B.Wr)
B.a17=s(["Severe epigastric pain boring through to the back, worse after fatty meals or alcohol, with nausea and vomiting.","Elevated serum lipase (more specific) and amylase confirm the diagnosis.","Care is rest for the gland: NPO initially, IV fluids, and effective analgesia.","Grey Turner sign (flank bruising) and Cullen sign (periumbilical bruising) indicate hemorrhagic pancreatitis.","Watch calcium; fat necrosis binds it and hypocalcemia may follow.","Alcohol abstinence and a low-fat diet are core discharge teaching."],t.s)
B.adx=new A.aw("Pancreatitis",B.a17)
B.WG=s([B.ae0,B.adp,B.adq,B.adx],t.u)
B.afd=new A.di("medsurg","GI & Liver Disorders",B.WG)
B.Wf=s(["Prerenal causes (hypovolemia, shock, heart failure) reduce perfusion and are the most common and most reversible.","Intrarenal causes damage tissue directly: nephrotoxins, contrast dye, prolonged ischemia.","Postrenal causes obstruct outflow: stones, enlarged prostate, tumors.","Oliguria is urine output below 400 mL/day; report output under 30 mL/hr in an adult.","During the oliguric phase watch for fluid overload and hyperkalemia; during diuresis watch for dehydration and hypokalemia.","Hold nephrotoxic drugs (NSAIDs, aminoglycosides, contrast) and dose-adjust renally cleared medications."],t.s)
B.adj=new A.aw("Acute Kidney Injury",B.Wf)
B.a1b=s(["CKD is progressive and irreversible; diabetes and hypertension are the leading causes.","Expect anemia from low erythropoietin; erythropoiesis-stimulating agents and iron are common treatments.","Renal diet typically restricts sodium, potassium, phosphorus, and fluid, with protein individualized to dialysis status.","Phosphate binders are taken with meals to work.","Uremic symptoms include pruritus, metallic taste, anorexia, and confusion as waste accumulates.","Avoid magnesium-containing antacids and laxatives in renal failure."],t.s)
B.aew=new A.aw("Chronic Kidney Disease",B.a1b)
B.XI=s(["Never take blood pressure, draw blood, or start IVs in the arm with an AV fistula or graft.","Assess fistula patency every shift: palpate a thrill and auscultate a bruit; absence of either is reported immediately.","Hold dialyzable and antihypertensive medications before hemodialysis per orders; hypotension during treatment is common.","Weigh before and after dialysis to quantify fluid removal.","Cloudy peritoneal dialysis effluent is the earliest sign of peritonitis; report it at once.","If peritoneal outflow slows, check tubing for kinks, reposition the client, and confirm the drainage bag is below the abdomen."],t.s)
B.aeQ=new A.aw("Dialysis Care",B.XI)
B.Wu=s(["Hyperkalemia: peaked T waves, wide QRS, muscle weakness; treat with calcium gluconate, insulin with dextrose, and kayexalate or dialysis.","Hypokalemia: flat T waves, U waves, cramps, dysrhythmias; never give potassium IV push.","Hyponatremia presents neurologically: headache, confusion, seizures; correct slowly.","Hypocalcemia: perioral tingling, positive Chvostek and Trousseau signs, laryngospasm risk.","Hypercalcemia: lethargy, constipation, kidney stones; hydrate and mobilize the client.","Fluid overload: bounding pulse, distended neck veins, crackles, edema; fluid deficit: tachycardia, hypotension, poor turgor, concentrated urine."],t.s)
B.ae6=new A.aw("Fluid & Electrolyte Alerts",B.Wu)
B.a_H=s([B.adj,B.aew,B.aeQ,B.ae6],t.u)
B.afe=new A.di("medsurg","Renal Disorders",B.a_H)
B.XW=s(["BE-FAST screening: balance, eyes, face droop, arm drift, speech change, time to call for help.","A non-contrast head CT must rule out hemorrhage before any thrombolytic is given.","Alteplase for ischemic stroke is generally given within 3 to 4.5 hours of symptom onset; last-known-well time is critical.","Left-hemisphere stroke often causes aphasia and right-sided weakness; right-hemisphere stroke causes impulsivity, left neglect, and spatial deficits.","Keep the client NPO until a swallow screen is passed; aspiration is a major early risk.","Homonymous hemianopsia: teach scanning toward the blind side and approach the client from the intact side initially."],t.s)
B.aeD=new A.aw("Stroke",B.XW)
B.WU=s(["During a seizure: lower the client to the floor, turn to the side, protect the head, loosen tight clothing, and time the event.","Never force anything into the mouth or restrain limbs during a seizure.","Status epilepticus (seizure over 5 minutes or repeated seizures without recovery) is an emergency; IV benzodiazepine is first line.","Seizure precautions: padded rails, suction and oxygen at bedside, IV access.","Document onset, duration, movement pattern, eye deviation, incontinence, and postictal state.","Phenytoin teaching: good oral hygiene for gum hyperplasia, no abrupt discontinuation, and levels drawn regularly (target about 10 to 20 mcg/mL)."],t.s)
B.aeR=new A.aw("Seizures",B.WU)
B.a1q=s(["Earliest ICP sign is a change in level of consciousness, before any vital sign change.","Cushing triad is late: widening pulse pressure, bradycardia, irregular respirations.","Keep head of bed at about 30 degrees with the head midline; avoid hip flexion, coughing, straining, and clustered care.","Hypertonic saline or mannitol pulls fluid from brain tissue; monitor serum osmolality and urine output.","Avoid lumbar puncture when ICP is markedly elevated because of herniation risk.","A fixed, dilated pupil on one side is an emergency sign of herniation."],t.s)
B.aeV=new A.aw("Increased Intracranial Pressure",B.a1q)
B.X3=s(["Parkinson triad: resting tremor (pill-rolling), rigidity, bradykinesia; add postural instability and shuffling gait.","Give carbidopa-levodopa on time, every time; delayed doses cause abrupt freezing and fall risk.","High-protein meals can blunt levodopa absorption; space protein away from doses if effect wears off.","Myasthenia gravis causes fluctuating weakness that worsens with activity; ptosis and diplopia are common first signs.","Myasthenic crisis (undermedication) and cholinergic crisis (overmedication) both cause severe weakness; edrophonium testing or clinical context distinguishes them, and airway support comes first.","Give pyridostigmine 30 to 45 minutes before meals so swallowing muscles are strongest while eating."],t.s)
B.aeE=new A.aw("Parkinson Disease & Myasthenia Gravis",B.X3)
B.XZ=s([B.aeD,B.aeR,B.aeV,B.aeE],t.u)
B.af4=new A.di("medsurg","Neuro Disorders",B.XZ)
B.a0s=s(["High Fowler (60 to 90 degrees) maximizes lung expansion for dyspnea and during meals for aspiration risk.","Orthopneic position (leaning forward over a table) helps COPD clients exhale trapped air.","Pulmonary edema: high Fowler with legs dependent to reduce venous return.","Unilateral lung disease: position with the good lung down to improve oxygenation.","After bronchoscopy, keep NPO and semi-Fowler until the gag reflex returns."],t.s)
B.aeP=new A.aw("Respiratory & Cardiac Positions",B.a0s)
B.Ym=s(["After lumbar puncture, lie flat as ordered to reduce spinal headache.","After femoral cardiac catheterization, keep the affected leg straight and the head of bed low per protocol.","After liver biopsy, lie on the right side with a pillow pressed against the site to tamponade bleeding.","Post-thyroidectomy: semi-Fowler with the head supported and neck in neutral alignment.","After a supratentorial craniotomy, keep the head of bed elevated about 30 degrees; after infratentorial surgery, keep flat per orders.","Following amputation, avoid prolonged hip flexion; periodic prone positioning prevents hip contracture with a leg amputation."],t.s)
B.aev=new A.aw("Post-Procedure Positions",B.Ym)
B.XG=s(["Air embolism from a central line: clamp the line and place the client on the left side in Trendelenburg.","Abdominal evisceration: low Fowler with knees flexed, cover organs with sterile saline-moistened gauze, and call the surgeon.","Autonomic dysreflexia (spinal injury above T6): sit the client upright first, then find and remove the trigger, usually a full bladder.","Shock without head injury: supine with legs modestly elevated.","Unconscious client without spine injury: lateral (recovery) position to protect the airway."],t.s)
B.adP=new A.aw("Surgical & Emergency Positions",B.XG)
B.WD=s(["Reposition dependent clients at least every 2 hours in bed and every hour in a chair.","A 30-degree lateral tilt keeps weight off the sacrum and trochanter simultaneously.","Keep heels floating off the mattress with a pillow under the calves.","Head of bed at or below 30 degrees (when allowed) reduces shear on the sacrum.","Use a lift sheet or device to move clients; dragging creates friction and shear injury."],t.s)
B.adm=new A.aw("Pressure Relief & Alignment",B.WD)
B.Vk=s([B.aeP,B.aev,B.adP,B.adm],t.u)
B.af2=new A.di("fundamentals","Positioning Guide",B.Vk)
B.X7=s(["X-ray confirmation is the gold standard for initial NG placement before anything is instilled.","Before each feeding or medication, verify placement (aspirate pH of 5 or less suggests gastric) and check residual per policy.","Keep the head of bed at least 30 degrees during and for an hour after feedings.","Flush with water before and after each medication; give drugs one at a time, fully dissolved.","For suction, low intermittent suction protects gastric mucosa with single-lumen tubes; a Salem sump vent must stay open to air.","New respiratory distress after insertion means possible tracheal placement; remove the tube."],t.s)
B.aeT=new A.aw("Nasogastric Tubes",B.X7)
B.Yt=s(["Insert with sterile technique; the drainage bag stays below bladder level and off the floor.","Keep tubing free of kinks and dependent loops; urine pooling in loops promotes infection.","Perform routine perineal and catheter care daily; do not routinely irrigate.","Remove indwelling catheters as soon as clinically possible; duration is the biggest CAUTI risk factor.","After removal, the client should void within 6 to 8 hours; bladder scan if not.","For continuous bladder irrigation after prostate surgery, titrate flow to keep outflow light pink and subtract irrigant from output."],t.s)
B.adu=new A.aw("Urinary Catheters",B.Yt)
B.Wv=s(["Gentle intermittent bubbling in the water-seal chamber with breathing is expected; continuous bubbling there means an air leak.","Tidaling (water level rising with inspiration) shows the system is patent; its absence may mean lung re-expansion or a kink.","Keep the drainage unit upright and below chest level; never lift it above the chest.","Do not clamp a chest tube during transport or routinely; clamping risks tension pneumothorax.","If the tube disconnects from the unit, submerge the tube end in sterile water; if it pulls out of the chest, cover with an occlusive dressing taped on three sides.","Report bright red drainage over 100 mL/hr, sudden dyspnea, tracheal deviation, or crepitus around the site."],t.s)
B.aef=new A.aw("Chest Tubes",B.Wv)
B.YB=s(["Keep an obturator, a spare tube of the same size, and one size smaller at the bedside at all times.","Suction only when indicated (coarse crackles, visible secretions, desaturation), not on a fixed schedule.","Apply suction only while withdrawing the catheter, for no more than 10 seconds, and preoxygenate first.","Use sterile technique for suctioning and inner cannula care in acute settings.","Secure ties so one to two fingers fit beneath them; have a second person hold the tube during tie changes.","If the tube dislodges within the first week, ventilate over the stoma or mouth and call for help; the tract may collapse."],t.s)
B.adE=new A.aw("Tracheostomy Care",B.YB)
B.a_q=s(["A healthy stoma is moist and pink-red; a pale, dusky, or black stoma means impaired blood flow and is reported immediately.","Empty the pouch when it is one-third to one-half full to prevent leaks and seal failure.","Cut the skin barrier opening only slightly larger than the stoma to protect surrounding skin.","Ileostomy output is liquid and continuous; these clients lose fluid and electrolytes quickly and must never take laxatives.","Colostomy irrigation (descending or sigmoid) can regulate elimination; use warm water and stop if cramping occurs.","Avoid enteric-coated and sustained-release oral drugs with an ileostomy; they may pass through unabsorbed."],t.s)
B.ae5=new A.aw("Ostomy Care",B.a_q)
B.WN=s([B.aeT,B.adu,B.aef,B.adE,B.ae5],t.u)
B.afa=new A.di("fundamentals","Tubes, Drains & Devices",B.WN)
B.Y_=s(["Stage 1: intact skin with nonblanchable redness over a bony prominence.","Stage 2: partial-thickness loss with a shallow open ulcer or intact serum-filled blister; no slough.","Stage 3: full-thickness loss with visible subcutaneous fat; may have slough, undermining, or tunneling.","Stage 4: full-thickness loss exposing bone, tendon, or muscle.","Unstageable: base obscured by slough or eschar, so depth cannot be determined.","Deep tissue injury: intact skin with persistent purple or maroon discoloration or a blood-filled blister.","Never massage reddened areas over bony prominences; it worsens tissue damage."],t.s)
B.adV=new A.aw("Pressure Injury Staging",B.Y_)
B.Zd=s(["Complete a risk score (such as Braden) on admission and per policy; low scores trigger a prevention bundle.","Reposition at least every 2 hours in bed; use pressure-redistribution mattresses for high-risk clients.","Keep skin clean and dry; moisture from incontinence macerates skin, so use barrier creams.","Adequate protein, calories, vitamin C, and zinc support tissue integrity and healing.","Inspect skin every shift, especially heels, sacrum, occiput, and under medical devices such as tubing and oxygen masks.","Minimize head-of-bed elevation beyond 30 degrees when possible to limit shear."],t.s)
B.ady=new A.aw("Prevention",B.Zd)
B.YY=s(["Measure length, width, and depth; probe gently for tunneling and undermining and document by clock position.","Healthy granulation tissue is beefy red and moist; yellow slough and black eschar require debridement to heal.","Increasing pain, warmth, purulent or foul drainage, and surrounding redness suggest infection.","Serous drainage is clear, sanguineous is bloody, serosanguineous is pink, purulent is thick and colored.","Dehiscence is separation of wound edges; evisceration adds protruding organs and is a surgical emergency.","A sudden gush of serosanguineous fluid from an abdominal incision often precedes dehiscence."],t.s)
B.aeS=new A.aw("Wound Assessment & Healing",B.YY)
B.a0b=s(["Match the dressing to the wound: keep a healing wound moist, absorb excess exudate, and fill dead space loosely.","Hydrocolloid dressings suit shallow, low-exudate wounds and can stay in place for several days.","Alginate dressings absorb heavy exudate; do not use them on dry wounds.","Do not debride stable, dry, intact heel eschar without provider direction; it can act as a natural cover.","Clean wounds from the least to the most contaminated area, using a new swab for each stroke.","With negative-pressure wound therapy, an intact seal is essential; report a nonfunctioning device promptly and monitor for bleeding."],t.s)
B.adg=new A.aw("Dressings & Debridement",B.a0b)
B.WH=s([B.adV,B.ady,B.aeS,B.adg],t.u)
B.af9=new A.di("fundamentals","Skin & Wound Care",B.WH)
B.a05=s(["Naegele rule: first day of the last menstrual period, subtract 3 months, add 7 days and a year.","Presumptive signs are what she feels (amenorrhea, nausea, quickening); probable signs are what the examiner finds; positive signs are fetal heartbeat, ultrasound visualization, and examiner-felt movement.","Expected weight gain for a normal-BMI woman is roughly 25 to 35 pounds.","Folic acid 400 mcg daily before and during early pregnancy prevents neural tube defects.","Avoid alcohol entirely, limit caffeine, and avoid raw or undercooked meats, unpasteurized dairy, and high-mercury fish.","From about 20 weeks, avoid flat supine lying; a side-lying or lateral-tilt position prevents vena cava compression."],t.s)
B.adn=new A.aw("Normal Pregnancy Basics",B.a05)
B.W4=s(["Any vaginal bleeding warrants evaluation; painless bright-red bleeding in late pregnancy suggests placenta previa.","Severe abdominal pain with a rigid uterus, with or without dark bleeding, suggests placental abruption.","Gush or steady leak of fluid before term suggests ruptured membranes; infection and cord prolapse are risks.","Severe headache, visual changes, epigastric pain, or sudden face and hand swelling suggest preeclampsia.","Fever, painful urination, or decreased fetal movement all need prompt evaluation.","Persistent vomiting with weight loss and ketones suggests hyperemesis gravidarum."],t.s)
B.ae_=new A.aw("Danger Signs to Report",B.W4)
B.a0z=s(["Preeclampsia: new hypertension at or above 140/90 after 20 weeks plus proteinuria or organ signs.","Severe features include BP at or above 160/110, headache, visual disturbance, epigastric pain, and rising liver enzymes or falling platelets (HELLP).","Magnesium sulfate prevents seizures; monitor respirations, deep tendon reflexes, and urine output during infusion.","Signs of magnesium toxicity: respirations under 12/min, absent reflexes, urine under 30 mL/hr; the antidote is calcium gluconate.","Maintain a quiet, low-stimulation room and seizure precautions.","Delivery of the placenta is the definitive cure."],t.s)
B.aeq=new A.aw("Preeclampsia & Eclampsia",B.a0z)
B.a_b=s(["Screen for gestational diabetes with a glucose challenge at 24 to 28 weeks.","Management starts with diet and exercise; insulin is added when glucose targets are not met (many oral agents are avoided).","GDM increases risk of macrosomia, birth trauma, and neonatal hypoglycemia.","A nonstress test is reactive (reassuring) when the fetal heart rate accelerates adequately with movement.","A biophysical profile scores fetal breathing, movement, tone, amniotic fluid, and the nonstress test.","Teach daily fetal kick counts in the third trimester; decreased movement is always reported."],t.s)
B.aee=new A.aw("Gestational Diabetes & Antepartum Testing",B.a_b)
B.UL=s([B.adn,B.ae_,B.aeq,B.aee],t.u)
B.afg=new A.di("maternity","Pregnancy & Antepartum",B.UL)
B.a0Y=s(["First stage runs from labor onset to full 10 cm dilation: latent (0 to 6 cm), active (6 to 10 cm), each with stronger, closer contractions.","Second stage is full dilation to birth; encourage pushing with contractions.","Third stage is birth to placental delivery, usually within 30 minutes; watch for the gush of blood and cord lengthening.","Fourth stage is the first hours after delivery; assess fundus, lochia, and vital signs frequently for hemorrhage.","True labor: regular contractions that intensify with walking and produce cervical change; false labor contractions are irregular and ease with rest.","Sudden nausea, trembling, and an urge to push often mark transition (8 to 10 cm)."],t.s)
B.aeI=new A.aw("Stages of Labor",B.a0Y)
B.a_D=s(["Normal baseline fetal heart rate is 110 to 160/min with moderate variability.","Accelerations are reassuring and reflect a well-oxygenated fetus.","Early decelerations mirror contractions and come from head compression; no intervention needed.","Variable decelerations are abrupt V or W shapes from cord compression; reposition the mother first.","Late decelerations begin after the contraction peak and signal uteroplacental insufficiency; they always require intervention.","Minimal or absent variability with recurrent late decelerations is a category III, ominous pattern.","Memory aid: cause pairs are head compression with early, cord compression with variable, placental insufficiency with late."],t.s)
B.adR=new A.aw("Fetal Heart Rate Patterns",B.a_D)
B.a0n=s(["For concerning tracings: turn the mother to her side, give an IV fluid bolus, apply oxygen, and stop oxytocin.","Notify the provider after starting corrective measures; prepare for possible operative delivery if no improvement.","Tachysystole is more than 5 contractions in 10 minutes averaged over 30 minutes; reduce or stop oxytocin.","Oxytocin infusions run on a pump as a piggyback line, titrated to contraction pattern and fetal response.","Contractions lasting over 90 seconds or resting tone that stays firm require stopping the infusion."],t.s)
B.aeN=new A.aw("Intrauterine Resuscitation & Oxytocin",B.a0n)
B.a01=s(["Prolapsed cord: relieve pressure by pushing the presenting part off the cord with a sterile gloved hand, place the mother knee-chest or deep Trendelenburg, and call for immediate delivery.","Never attempt to push a prolapsed cord back in; keep exposed cord moist with saline gauze.","Shoulder dystocia: McRoberts maneuver (sharp hip flexion) plus suprapubic pressure; never apply fundal pressure.","Uterine rupture: sudden tearing pain, loss of fetal station, abnormal fetal heart rate, maternal shock; emergency cesarean.","Amniotic fluid embolism: sudden dyspnea, hypotension, and coagulopathy; provide full resuscitation support."],t.s)
B.ado=new A.aw("Labor Emergencies",B.a01)
B.a0f=s([B.aeI,B.adR,B.aeN,B.ado],t.u)
B.af8=new A.di("maternity","Labor, Delivery & Fetal Monitoring",B.a0f)
B.We=s(["Immediately after birth the fundus is firm at about the umbilicus, then descends roughly one fingerbreadth per day.","A boggy fundus gets massaged first; if it deviates to one side, have the client empty her bladder.","Lochia progresses rubra (red, days 1 to 3), serosa (pink-brown), alba (white-yellow); a return to bright red is reported.","Saturating a pad in an hour or less, or passing large clots, indicates hemorrhage.","Assess breasts, uterus, bladder, bowel, lochia, episiotomy or incision, extremities (for thrombosis), and emotional status each shift.","Unilateral calf pain, warmth, or swelling suggests deep vein thrombosis; do not massage the leg."],t.s)
B.aeA=new A.aw("Postpartum Assessment",B.We)
B.WC=s(["Uterine atony causes most early hemorrhage; first actions are fundal massage and bladder emptying.","Uterotonics after massage include oxytocin, methylergonovine (avoid with hypertension), and carboprost (avoid with asthma).","Persistent trickling with a firm fundus suggests a laceration rather than atony.","Postpartum infection signs: fever above 38\xb0 C after the first 24 hours, foul lochia, uterine tenderness.","Postpartum blues resolve within about 2 weeks; symptoms beyond that, anhedonia, or thoughts of harming self or infant indicate postpartum depression and need referral.","Any statement about harming the baby or psychotic symptoms is an emergency."],t.s)
B.aeh=new A.aw("Postpartum Hemorrhage & Complications",B.WC)
B.Yy=s(["APGAR scores heart rate, respiratory effort, muscle tone, reflex irritability, and color at 1 and 5 minutes; 7 to 10 is reassuring.","Normal newborn vitals: heart rate 110 to 160/min, respirations 30 to 60/min, temperature kept 36.5 to 37.5\xb0 C.","Prevent heat loss immediately: dry, skin-to-skin or warmer, and a hat; cold stress raises oxygen and glucose demand.","Acrocyanosis (blue hands and feet) is normal early; central cyanosis is not.","Standard newborn medications: vitamin K IM for clotting and erythromycin eye ointment.","Expected findings include molding, milia, vernix, and lanugo; a bulging fontanel at rest is not expected."],t.s)
B.adf=new A.aw("Newborn Transition & APGAR",B.Yy)
B.YK=s(["Newborns at risk for hypoglycemia (of diabetic mothers, large or small for age, preterm) need glucose checks; jitteriness is a key sign.","Breastfed newborns eat every 2 to 3 hours; adequate intake shows as 6 or more wet diapers daily after day 4 and steady weight gain after initial loss.","Proper latch takes in the nipple and much of the areola; sore nipples usually mean a shallow latch.","Place infants supine for every sleep, on a firm surface, with no loose bedding or bed sharing.","Keep the umbilical cord dry and open to air; report redness, drainage, or foul odor.","Jaundice within the first 24 hours is pathologic; jaundice appearing after 24 hours is common and monitored with bilirubin levels."],t.s)
B.aeF=new A.aw("Newborn Care & Feeding",B.YK)
B.VQ=s([B.aeA,B.aeh,B.adf,B.aeF],t.u)
B.afj=new A.di("maternity","Postpartum & Newborn",B.VQ)
B.W6=s(["Social smile around 2 months; head steady and hands to midline by 4 months.","Rolls front to back around 4 to 5 months; sits without support around 6 to 8 months.","Birth weight roughly doubles by 6 months and triples by 12 months.","Pincer grasp and pulling to stand emerge around 9 to 10 months; first words near 12 months.","Stranger anxiety beginning around 6 to 8 months is normal development, not a red flag.","Posterior fontanel closes by about 2 months; anterior closes by about 18 months.","Introduce solid foods around 6 months; no honey before 12 months because of botulism risk."],t.s)
B.aeu=new A.aw("Infant (Birth to 12 Months)",B.W6)
B.WE=s(["Walks independently by about 15 months; runs and kicks a ball by 2 years.","Two-word phrases around 2 years; vocabulary explodes during this stage.","Parallel play (alongside, not with, other children) is characteristic.",'Temper tantrums and constant "no" reflect Erikson stage of autonomy versus shame and doubt.','Offer simple choices ("red cup or blue cup") to support autonomy and reduce power struggles.',"Toilet training readiness cues, not age, guide timing: dry periods, walking, and interest.","Toddlers are the highest-risk group for poisoning and drowning; safety-proofing is priority teaching."],t.s)
B.ads=new A.aw("Toddler (1 to 3 Years)",B.WE)
B.Y5=s(["Preschoolers (3 to 5) engage in associative and imaginative play; magical thinking makes them believe illness can be punishment.","Explain procedures to preschoolers with play, simple words, and equipment they can touch, just before the event.","Preschool fears center on body mutilation; adhesive bandages are surprisingly therapeutic.","School-age children (6 to 12) think concretely, enjoy rules and cooperative games, and are in industry versus inferiority.","School-age children want to understand how things work; use models, diagrams, and honest simple explanations.","Peer comparison begins in school age; chronic illness care plans should protect participation with peers."],t.s)
B.adJ=new A.aw("Preschool & School Age",B.Y5)
B.a_Z=s(["Adolescents work through identity versus role confusion; peers are the dominant influence.","Interview teens privately for part of the visit; confidentiality (within safety limits) improves honesty.","Body image concerns dominate; illness or treatment that alters appearance hits hardest at this age.","Abstract thinking develops, but risk-taking peaks; injury is the leading threat to adolescent health.","Screen for mood changes, substance use, and suicidal thoughts at routine visits.","Support decision-making participation in their own care to build adherence."],t.s)
B.aec=new A.aw("Adolescent",B.a_Z)
B.a_a=s([B.aeu,B.ads,B.adJ,B.aec],t.u)
B.aeW=new A.di("peds","Growth & Development Milestones",B.a_a)
B.Zj=s(["Croup: barky seal-like cough, inspiratory stridor, hoarseness, usually viral and worse at night.","Mild croup responds to cool humidified air; stridor at rest gets nebulized epinephrine and corticosteroids.","Epiglottitis: sudden high fever, drooling, dysphagia, distress, and tripod positioning; suspect in the unimmunized child.","With suspected epiglottitis, never inspect the throat with a tongue blade or take an oral temperature; airway may close.","Keep the child calm on a caregiver lap and prepare for emergency airway management by skilled personnel.","Hib vaccination has made epiglottitis rare; ask about immunization status."],t.s)
B.add=new A.aw("Croup & Epiglottitis",B.Zj)
B.a0B=s(["RSV bronchiolitis peaks in infants: copious nasal secretions, wheezing, crackles, retractions, and feeding difficulty.","Care is supportive: nasal suctioning before feeds and sleep, hydration, oxygen for hypoxemia.","Antibiotics do not treat RSV; it is viral.","Contact plus standard precautions limit spread; strict hand hygiene is essential.","Apnea can be the presenting sign in very young or preterm infants.","Red flags for the caregiver: nasal flaring, grunting, retractions, fewer wet diapers, and cyanosis."],t.s)
B.aeK=new A.aw("Bronchiolitis & RSV",B.a0B)
B.WZ=s(["Pediatric asthma signs include nighttime cough and exercise intolerance, not just wheezing.","Use a spacer with metered-dose inhalers in children to improve lung delivery.","A quiet chest with poor air entry in an asthmatic child signals impending failure.","Cystic fibrosis produces thick secretions: chronic productive cough, recurrent lung infections, and fat malabsorption with greasy stools.","CF care includes airway clearance therapy, pancreatic enzymes with every meal and snack, and a high-calorie, high-protein diet with fat-soluble vitamin (A, D, E, K) supplements.","Salty-tasting skin and meconium ileus at birth are classic CF clues; sweat chloride testing confirms."],t.s)
B.aeH=new A.aw("Asthma & Cystic Fibrosis",B.WZ)
B.XM=s(["Defects with increased pulmonary blood flow (VSD, ASD, PDA) cause heart failure signs: poor feeding, sweating with feeds, poor weight gain, tachypnea.","Tetralogy of Fallot causes cyanosis; hypercyanotic (tet) spells are treated by placing the infant in knee-chest position and giving oxygen.","Toddlers with cyanotic defects may squat instinctively to improve pulmonary blood flow.","Coarctation of the aorta: higher blood pressure and stronger pulses in the arms than the legs.","Feeding an infant in heart failure: small frequent feeds, limited feeding time, high-calorie formula as ordered.","Count a full minute apical pulse before digoxin and follow pediatric hold parameters."],t.s)
B.adO=new A.aw("Congenital Heart Defects",B.XM)
B.X2=s([B.add,B.aeK,B.aeH,B.adO],t.u)
B.aeZ=new A.di("peds","Pediatric Respiratory & Cardiac",B.X2)
B.a1h=s(["Pyloric stenosis: projectile nonbilious vomiting in the first weeks of life, a hungry baby, and an olive-shaped mass in the right upper abdomen.","Vomiting in pyloric stenosis causes hypochloremic metabolic alkalosis; correct fluids and electrolytes before surgery.","Intussusception: sudden episodic drawing-up-the-knees abdominal pain, sausage-shaped abdominal mass, and currant-jelly stools (late sign).","An air or contrast enema often reduces intussusception; passage of a normal brown stool may mean spontaneous reduction and must be reported.","Bilious (green) vomiting in an infant suggests obstruction such as malrotation with volvulus; it is an emergency."],t.s)
B.aeB=new A.aw("Pyloric Stenosis & Intussusception",B.a1h)
B.XF=s(["Weigh the child; weight change is the best measure of fluid loss, and daily weights track therapy.","Dehydration signs in infants: sunken fontanel, no tears, dry mucous membranes, fewer wet diapers, delayed capillary refill.","Mild to moderate dehydration is treated with oral rehydration solution in small frequent amounts.","Avoid plain water, juice, and soda for rehydration; they lack proper electrolytes and can worsen diarrhea.","Severe dehydration (lethargy, tachycardia, poor perfusion) needs isotonic IV boluses.","Resume an age-appropriate normal diet as soon as rehydrated; prolonged clear liquids are unnecessary."],t.s)
B.adG=new A.aw("Dehydration & Gastroenteritis",B.XF)
B.Uy=s(["First step for any ingestion: call poison control; do not induce vomiting.","Acetaminophen overdose can be silent early and destroy the liver; N-acetylcysteine is the antidote.","Iron tablet ingestion is a leading cause of fatal childhood poisoning; GI bleeding and shock can follow.","Button batteries lodged in the esophagus burn through tissue within hours; this is an immediate emergency.","Lead exposure causes developmental delay and anemia; screen children in older housing.","Store all medications and cleaning products locked and in original containers; never call medicine candy."],t.s)
B.adU=new A.aw("Poisoning & Ingestions",B.Uy)
B.Yh=s(["Any rectal temperature of 38\xb0 C (100.4\xb0 F) or higher in an infant under 3 months requires urgent evaluation.","Febrile seizures are typically brief, generalized, and occur with rapid temperature rise; protect from injury and reassure caregivers.","Never give aspirin to children with viral illness because of Reye syndrome risk.","Dose pediatric acetaminophen and ibuprofen by weight, and use only the measuring device supplied.","A child who suddenly cannot speak or cough while eating needs abdominal thrusts (back blows and chest thrusts for infants under 1 year).","Car seats: rear-facing as long as the seat allows, and children under 13 ride in the back seat."],t.s)
B.aey=new A.aw("Fever, Seizures & Safety",B.Yh)
B.Y0=s([B.aeB,B.adG,B.adU,B.aey],t.u)
B.afc=new A.di("peds","Pediatric GI & Emergencies",B.Y0)
B.XT=s(['Open-ended prompts ("Tell me more about that") invite the client to lead.','Reflection returns the feeling ("You sound frustrated") to show understanding.',"Restating paraphrases content so the client can confirm or correct it.","Silence gives space to think; sitting quietly with a client is an intervention.",'Offering self ("I will stay with you") communicates worth without demands.',"Clarifying and validating check meaning instead of assuming it.","Focus on feelings first; on the exam, the option acknowledging the client's emotion usually outranks giving information."],t.s)
B.adz=new A.aw("Techniques That Work",B.XT)
B.ZM=s(['False reassurance ("Everything will be fine") dismisses real concerns.','Asking "why" puts clients on the defensive.','Giving advice ("If I were you...") removes the client from decision-making.',"Changing the subject signals the topic is unwelcome.","Approving or disapproving makes the nurse the judge of the client's choices.","Closed yes/no questions shut down exploration when feelings matter.",'Minimizing ("Others have it worse") invalidates the client\'s experience.'],t.s)
B.ae8=new A.aw("Blocks to Avoid",B.ZM)
B.Yx=s(['For hallucinations: do not argue or play along; acknowledge the client\'s experience while presenting reality ("I do not hear the voices, but I believe they are frightening for you").',"For delusions: avoid debating the false belief; respond to the underlying feeling and redirect to reality-based activity.",'Ask directly about suicide ("Are you thinking of killing yourself?"); asking does not plant the idea.',"With angry or escalating clients: stay calm, keep distance and an open exit, use a low voice, and set clear limits.","With cognitive impairment: short sentences, one instruction at a time, and no quizzing about facts they cannot recall.","Use professional interpreters for language barriers, never family members, for clinical discussions."],t.s)
B.adk=new A.aw("Special Situations",B.Yx)
B.XY=s([B.adz,B.ae8,B.adk],t.u)
B.af1=new A.di("mental","Therapeutic Communication",B.XY)
B.Xh=s(["Major depression: at least 2 weeks of depressed mood or anhedonia plus sleep, appetite, energy, concentration, or worth changes.","Highest-risk warning signs: a specific plan with available means, giving away possessions, and sudden calm after despair.","Sudden improvement in mood may mean the decision to die has been made; increase, not decrease, vigilance.","A client with a plan and means requires one-to-one observation and a safe environment (remove cords, sharps, glass).","Energy often returns before mood lifts early in antidepressant therapy, raising suicide risk.","Ask about suicidal ideation directly and document the client's exact words."],t.s)
B.ae3=new A.aw("Depression & Suicide Risk",B.Xh)
B.WA=s(["Mania: elevated or irritable mood, grandiosity, pressured speech, flight of ideas, decreased need for sleep, and reckless spending or sexual behavior.","Manic clients may not eat or sleep; offer high-calorie finger foods and fluids they can consume on the move.","Provide a low-stimulation environment and brief, direct communication.","Set consistent limits on intrusive or unsafe behavior; do not negotiate them.","Channel energy into gross motor, noncompetitive activity such as walking.","Protect the client from the long-term consequences of impulsivity (financial, sexual, legal)."],t.s)
B.ae1=new A.aw("Bipolar Disorder & Mania",B.WA)
B.Yu=s(["Positive symptoms add experiences: hallucinations, delusions, disorganized speech and behavior.","Negative symptoms subtract: flat affect, alogia, avolition, social withdrawal; they respond less well to medication.","Command hallucinations telling the client to harm self or others are a safety emergency; assess content directly.","Build trust with brief, frequent, consistent contacts; avoid touch without warning.","Medication nonadherence is the most common cause of relapse; long-acting injectables help.","Monitor antipsychotic clients for EPS, metabolic changes, and NMS."],t.s)
B.adS=new A.aw("Schizophrenia",B.Yu)
B.WM=s(["A client in panic-level anxiety cannot process teaching; stay with them, use short simple phrases, and reduce stimulation.","Teach slow diaphragmatic breathing for panic attack symptoms once the peak passes.","PTSD features intrusive re-experiencing, avoidance, negative mood shifts, and hyperarousal after trauma; ground the client during flashbacks by orienting to the present.","Never force a trauma survivor to recount details before they are ready.","Crisis intervention is short-term and goal-directed: safety first, then return to pre-crisis functioning.","In any behavioral emergency the sequence is least restrictive first: verbal de-escalation, then medication offer, with restraint or seclusion only as a last resort under strict orders and monitoring."],t.s)
B.aed=new A.aw("Anxiety, PTSD & Crisis Intervention",B.WM)
B.Z2=s(["Alcohol withdrawal starts within hours: tremor, sweating, tachycardia, anxiety; seizures may occur in the first 48 hours.","Delirium tremens (disorientation, hallucinations, autonomic storm) typically appears 48 to 96 hours after the last drink and can be fatal.","Benzodiazepines on a symptom-triggered scale (such as CIWA) are the mainstay of alcohol withdrawal treatment.","Give thiamine before or with glucose in alcohol-dependent clients to prevent Wernicke encephalopathy.","Opioid withdrawal is miserable but rarely dangerous: yawning, rhinorrhea, cramps, diarrhea, gooseflesh; treat symptomatically or with agonist therapy.","Naloxone reverses opioid overdose; repeat doses may be needed because it wears off before many opioids."],t.s)
B.aeU=new A.aw("Substance Withdrawal",B.Z2)
B.Zh=s([B.ae3,B.ae1,B.adS,B.aed,B.aeU],t.u)
B.afh=new A.di("mental","Psychiatric Disorders & Crisis Care",B.Zh)
B.Uc=s(["Airway beats breathing, breathing beats circulation; an obstructed airway is always the first priority.","An actual airway problem outranks a potential one; stridor beats a client at risk for aspiration.","After ABCs, immediate safety threats (suicide risk, seizure, violence) come next.","Circulation problems include active hemorrhage and shock, not just cardiac rhythm.","Exception logic: with sudden massive bleeding or CPR (circulation-first resuscitation), control of circulation is addressed per current life-support sequence."],t.s)
B.adT=new A.aw("ABC & Safety First",B.Uc)
B.Xx=s(["Physiologic needs (oxygen, fluid, nutrition, elimination, temperature) come before safety, and safety before psychosocial needs.","A physical need generally outranks an emotional one, but an emotional crisis with a safety risk (suicidal intent) rises to the top.","Assess before you act: gather data before intervening unless the situation demands immediate action.",'In an emergency the "assess first" rule yields to lifesaving intervention (for example, ventricular fibrillation gets defibrillation, not more assessment).',"Pain is real and important, but it rarely outranks airway, breathing, circulation, or acute deterioration."],t.s)
B.aer=new A.aw("Maslow & Nursing Process",B.Xx)
B.WL=s(["Acute, new-onset problems outrank chronic, expected ones.","Unexpected findings for a diagnosis outrank expected findings; crackles are expected in heart failure but new tracheal deviation is not expected in pneumothorax care.","Unstable clients (changing vitals, new confusion, worsening trend) are seen before stable clients.","A post-operative client in the first 24 hours is treated as higher risk than one several days out.","Trending matters: a saturation of 91 percent and falling outranks a stable 89 percent in a COPD client at baseline."],t.s)
B.aek=new A.aw("Acute vs Chronic, Stable vs Unstable",B.WL)
B.a1p=s(["See first the client whose finding suggests airway compromise, hemorrhage, or acute neurologic change.","Signs suggesting deterioration (restlessness, new confusion, rising respiratory rate) beat abnormal but expected labs.","A client screaming in pain has an airway; the quiet, obtunded client may not.","Discharge teaching, routine medications, and documentation can be delayed; a changing assessment cannot.","When two clients are unstable, choose the one with the most lethal, most reversible problem."],t.s)
B.adN=new A.aw("Who Do You See First?",B.a1p)
B.WP=s([B.adT,B.aer,B.aek,B.adN],t.u)
B.af5=new A.di("leadership","Prioritization Frameworks",B.WP)
B.a0p=s(["Initial and admission assessments, and any assessment of an unstable client, stay with the RN.","Care planning, nursing diagnosis, evaluation of outcomes, and clinical judgment cannot be delegated.","Client teaching and discharge education are RN functions; others may reinforce established teaching.","IV push medications, blood product initiation, and titrating drips are RN tasks in most jurisdictions.","The RN takes the first assessment of a newly admitted, post-operative, or deteriorating client.","Delegation transfers the task, not the accountability; the RN remains answerable for outcomes."],t.s)
B.adY=new A.aw("RN-Only Responsibilities",B.a0p)
B.ZQ=s(["LPNs care for stable clients with predictable outcomes.","LPNs may collect data (vital signs, focused observations) and report findings, but the RN interprets and synthesizes.","LPNs typically give oral, subcutaneous, and IM medications; IV scope varies by state and facility.","LPNs can perform sterile procedures such as wound care, catheter insertion, and tracheostomy suctioning of established airways.","LPNs may reinforce teaching the RN has already provided.","LPNs do not do initial assessments, develop care plans, or take verbal orders in many settings; know facility policy."],t.s)
B.ae9=new A.aw("What the LPN Can Do",B.ZQ)
B.Z4=s(["UAP tasks are routine, standardized, and for stable clients: hygiene, ambulation, turning, feeding clients without swallowing risk.","UAP may take and record vital signs and intake and output on stable clients; the nurse interprets the numbers.","UAP may collect specimens they are trained for and measure blood glucose per facility policy.","Never delegate to a UAP: assessment, teaching, medication administration, or anything requiring judgment.","Do not assign a UAP to feed a client with dysphagia or ambulate a client for the first time post-operatively.","Instructions to the UAP must be specific: what to do, when, and exactly what to report back."],t.s)
B.aeg=new A.aw("What the UAP Can Do",B.Z4)
B.Xo=s(["Right task: routine, low-risk, within the delegatee's validated skills.","Right circumstance: the client is stable and the setting appropriate.","Right person: matched to the specific client and task, not just the job title.","Right direction: clear, concise, specific expectations, including what to report.","Right supervision: the RN monitors, evaluates, and intervenes as needed, then follows up on results.","When a delegatee reports an abnormal finding, the RN reassesses personally before acting on it."],t.s)
B.aeC=new A.aw("The Five Rights of Delegation",B.Xo)
B.a_V=s([B.adY,B.ae9,B.aeg,B.aeC],t.u)
B.af0=new A.di("leadership","Delegation & Scope of Practice",B.a_V)
B.a1j=s(["Standard precautions apply to every client, every time, regardless of diagnosis.","Hand hygiene before and after every client contact is the single most effective infection control measure.","Use soap and water (not alcohol rub) when hands are visibly soiled and after C. difficile or norovirus contact.","Wear gloves for any anticipated contact with blood, body fluids, or nonintact skin; add gown, mask, or eye protection as splash risk dictates.","Never recap used needles; dispose of sharps immediately in the container at the point of use."],t.s)
B.aen=new A.aw("Standard Precautions",B.a1j)
B.Wq=s(["Contact precautions: gown and gloves for room entry; private room or cohorting.","Key contact organisms and conditions: MRSA, VRE, C. difficile, scabies, RSV, norovirus, and major draining wounds.","Use dedicated or disposable equipment (stethoscope, BP cuff) that stays in the room.","Remove gown and gloves and perform hand hygiene before leaving the room.","For C. difficile, soap-and-water handwashing and sporicidal cleaning are required; alcohol does not kill spores."],t.s)
B.adF=new A.aw("Contact Precautions",B.Wq)
B.a1_=s(["Droplet precautions add a surgical mask within 3 feet (about 1 meter) of the client; droplets fall quickly and do not linger in air.","Key droplet illnesses: influenza, pertussis, mumps, rubella, meningococcal meningitis, streptococcal pharyngitis, and mycoplasma pneumonia.","Private room preferred; the client wears a surgical mask during any transport.","Meningococcal clients may come off droplet precautions after 24 hours of effective antibiotics.",'Memory anchor: droplet diseases are mostly "coughing and kissing" respiratory and pharyngeal infections.'],t.s)
B.ael=new A.aw("Droplet Precautions",B.a1_)
B.TU=s(["Airborne precautions require a negative-pressure room with the door closed and an N95 respirator (fit-tested) or PAPR for staff.","The airborne trio: tuberculosis, measles (rubeola), and varicella (chickenpox), plus disseminated zoster.","The client wears a surgical mask, not an N95, during transport outside the room.","For varicella and measles, assign immune caregivers when possible.","Airborne particles remain suspended and travel on air currents, which is why a regular mask is insufficient for staff."],t.s)
B.adr=new A.aw("Airborne Precautions",B.TU)
B.a06=s(["Donning order: gown, mask or respirator, goggles or face shield, gloves.","Doffing order: gloves, goggles or face shield, gown, mask or respirator; the respirator comes off outside the room.","Gloves are the most contaminated item and always come off first.","Perform hand hygiene immediately after removing all PPE, and any time hands are contaminated mid-doffing.","Neutropenic (protective) isolation is the reverse concern: protect the client, so no fresh flowers, raw produce per policy, no ill visitors, and meticulous hand hygiene."],t.s)
B.adK=new A.aw("PPE On & Off Order",B.a06)
B.ZB=s([B.aen,B.adF,B.ael,B.adr,B.adK],t.u)
B.aeX=new A.di("safety","Isolation Precautions",B.ZB)
B.Y9=s(["RACE order: Rescue anyone in immediate danger, Activate the alarm, Contain the fire (close doors), Extinguish or Evacuate.","PASS for extinguishers: Pull the pin, Aim at the base of the fire, Squeeze the handle, Sweep side to side.","Evacuate horizontally first (past fire doors on the same floor); vertical evacuation comes later if needed.","Move ambulatory clients first in an evacuation, then wheelchair users, then bedbound clients.","Turn off oxygen in the fire zone per protocol; know the shutoff valve locations on your unit.","Never use elevators during a fire."],t.s)
B.adI=new A.aw("Fire Safety: RACE & PASS",B.Y9)
B.WV=s(["For an unresponsive, pulseless client: call for help, start high-quality compressions immediately, and attach the AED or defibrillator as soon as it arrives.","Compressions are at least 100 to 120 per minute, 2 to 2.4 inches deep in adults, with full recoil and minimal interruptions.","Defibrillate shockable rhythms (ventricular fibrillation, pulseless VT) as early as possible; every minute of delay reduces survival.","Disaster triage reverses everyday logic: greatest good for the greatest number, so resources go to salvageable clients.","Common triage tags: red is immediate (life-threatening but survivable), yellow is delayed, green is walking wounded, black is expectant or deceased.","A disaster victim who is not breathing after one airway repositioning is tagged black."],t.s)
B.adw=new A.aw("Codes & Disaster Triage",B.WV)
B.UJ=s(["At seizure onset: ease the client to the floor or bed, turn the head or body to the side, and clear hard objects away.","Protect the head with padding; loosen anything tight around the neck.","Do not restrain movements and never place anything in the mouth.","Time the seizure; more than 5 minutes or back-to-back seizures means status epilepticus and an emergency response.","Afterward, keep side-lying, check breathing, reorient the client, and document the event in detail.","Bedside setup for at-risk clients: suction, oxygen, padded rails, and IV access."],t.s)
B.adW=new A.aw("Seizure Safety",B.UJ)
B.a_m=s(["Screen every client for fall risk on admission and after any change in condition or medication.","Universal measures: bed low and locked, call light and personal items in reach, nonslip footwear, clutter-free path, adequate lighting.","Hourly rounding that addresses toileting, pain, and positioning prevents the impulsive solo trips that cause falls.","Do not use full side rails as a barrier; four raised rails can act as a restraint and increase injury from climbing over.","Restraints are a last resort: least restrictive device, a provider order within the required timeframe, never PRN, and scheduled checks with circulation, skin, and range-of-motion care.","Secure restraint ties to the bed frame with a quick-release knot, never to the side rails."],t.s)
B.adi=new A.aw("Fall Prevention & Restraints",B.a_m)
B.a_N=s([B.adI,B.adw,B.adW,B.adi],t.u)
B.af6=new A.di("safety","Emergency Response",B.a_N)
B.W_=s([B.af7,B.aff,B.afb,B.af_,B.af3,B.afi,B.aeY,B.afk,B.afd,B.afe,B.af4,B.af2,B.afa,B.af9,B.afg,B.af8,B.afj,B.aeW,B.aeZ,B.afc,B.af1,B.afh,B.af5,B.af0,B.aeX,B.af6],t.XI)
