B.asO=new A.eo('Adult, first day after abdominal surgery. The abdominal dressing is saturated with fresh blood and the patient feels "dizzy."',118,"88/54",24,96,37.2,B.cW,'Tachycardia with falling blood pressure plus visible fresh bleeding is hemorrhagic shock until proven otherwise \u2014 activate the rapid response team while applying pressure and staying with the patient. Waiting to "monitor" a compensating patient loses the window before decompensation.')
B.asX=new A.eo("Older adult with long-standing COPD on 2 L oxygen, comfortable and talking in full sentences, at their usual baseline.",88,"132/78",20,90,36.9,B.dQ,'A saturation of 88 to 92 percent is the accepted target for many COPD patients, and this one is comfortable and at baseline \u2014 routine monitoring is appropriate. Chasing a "normal" saturation with more oxygen can suppress their respiratory drive.')
B.asQ=new A.eo("Young adult two hours after IV opioid analgesia. Very drowsy, answers questions slowly, then drifts back to sleep.",74,"112/68",7,89,36.8,B.cW,"A respiratory rate of 7 with falling saturation after opioids is respiratory depression \u2014 an airway emergency. Stimulate the patient, call the rapid response team, and prepare naloxone; this cannot wait for a routine provider callback.")
B.asH=new A.eo("Middle-aged adult admitted with pneumonia. Now lethargic and difficult to keep awake, skin warm and flushed.",122,"92/58",26,93,39.4,B.cW,"Fever, tachycardia, tachypnea, hypotension, and new lethargy in an infected patient meet sepsis criteria \u2014 this is progressing toward septic shock and needs the rapid response team, cultures, and early antibiotics, not routine monitoring.")
B.asG=new A.eo("Young adult waiting to go to a procedure, wringing their hands and asking many questions. No pain, no shortness of breath.",104,"138/84",18,99,36.7,B.dQ,"Mild tachycardia and elevated blood pressure with normal saturation in an anxious but otherwise well patient is a stress response. Therapeutic communication and reassurance come first; recheck after the patient settles.")
B.asK=new A.eo('Older adult admitted with a urinary tract infection. The family says they are "not themselves today" \u2014 new confusion this shift.',96,"118/70",20,95,38.2,B.en,"New confusion in an older adult with infection is an early warning of urosepsis or delirium even when vital signs look acceptable. Do a focused assessment and notify the provider now \u2014 do not wait for the numbers to deteriorate.")
B.asI=new A.eo("Adult reporting crushing chest pressure rated 7 out of 10, radiating to the jaw, skin cool and diaphoretic.",92,"150/90",20,97,36.9,B.cW,"Crushing chest pain with diaphoresis is treated as myocardial infarction until ruled out, even with stable-looking vitals. Activate the emergency response, obtain a 12-lead ECG, and stay with the patient \u2014 time is muscle.")
B.asJ=new A.eo("Child recovering from a tonsillectomy who keeps swallowing repeatedly and clearing their throat while dozing.",108,"104/64",22,98,37,B.en,"Frequent swallowing after tonsillectomy is the classic early sign of bleeding at the surgical site, often before any blood is visible. Inspect the throat, put the child on their side, and notify the surgeon promptly.")
B.asF=new A.eo('Adult on hemodialysis who missed their last two sessions. Feels "heavy and weak." The cardiac monitor shows tall, peaked T waves with an irregular, slow rhythm.',48,"104/62",18,96,36.6,B.cW,"Missed dialysis plus peaked T waves and bradycardia means severe hyperkalemia \u2014 one step from cardiac arrest. Call the rapid response team and prepare calcium gluconate, insulin with dextrose, and urgent dialysis.")
B.asV=new A.eo("Adult one day after knee replacement, doing breathing exercises. Mild incision pain controlled with oral analgesia.",82,"124/76",16,97,37.4,B.dQ,"These vital signs are within expected postoperative limits, and a low-grade temperature on day one usually reflects the normal inflammatory response and atelectasis \u2014 encourage the incentive spirometer and continue routine monitoring.")
B.asD=new A.eo("Adult marathon runner admitted the evening before a scheduled ankle repair. Relaxed and watching TV; denies dizziness, chest pain, or fatigue.",48,"114/70",12,99,36.6,B.dQ,"A resting heart rate of 48 is expected in a well-conditioned endurance athlete, whose larger stroke volume needs fewer beats. Blood pressure is normal and there is no dizziness or fatigue, so the bradycardia is not symptomatic \u2014 document it as baseline. The same rate with hypotension or confusion would need escalation.")
B.asW=new A.eo("Client at 32 weeks of pregnancy at a routine prenatal visit. Feels well; no headache, visual changes, or new swelling.",96,"112/68",18,98,36.8,B.dQ,"Blood volume rises by up to 50 percent in pregnancy, and the resting heart rate normally climbs 10 to 20 beats per minute, so a pulse of 96 with normal blood pressure is expected. Nothing here suggests preeclampsia. A blood pressure of 140/90 or higher, or a headache with visual changes, would need prompt notification.")
B.asR=new A.eo("Adult two hours after surgical repair of a forearm fracture, grimacing and rating pain 8 out of 10; the next analgesic dose is due now. Fingers are warm and pink with brisk capillary refill and normal sensation.",106,"148/88",22,97,37.1,B.dQ,"Acute pain triggers a sympathetic response, so a mildly raised heart rate, blood pressure, and respiratory rate fit a pain score of 8, and the neurovascular checks are normal. Give the due analgesic, elevate the arm, and recheck vitals and pain within an hour. Pain that does not respond to analgesia, or changing sensation, would point to compartment syndrome and need the surgeon.")
B.asT=new A.eo("Two-year-old admitted overnight for mild dehydration, now drinking well and playing with toys in the crib.",118,"96/58",28,98,37.2,B.dQ,"Normal ranges are higher in young children: an awake toddler's heart rate runs about 98 to 140 and respiratory rate about 22 to 37. These values fit a playful, well-hydrated 2-year-old \u2014 adult limits would wrongly flag tachycardia and tachypnea. Lethargy or refusing fluids would change the picture.")
B.asM=new A.eo("Adult with cellulitis of the lower leg on IV antibiotics since yesterday. Respiratory rate has climbed from 16 to 24 over four hours, and the client is newly slow to answer and unsure of the day.",104,"118/72",24,95,38.3,B.en,"A rising respiratory rate and new confusion in a client with an infection are early signs of sepsis that often appear before the blood pressure falls. Do a focused assessment and notify the provider now \u2014 anticipate a lactate level, blood cultures, and fluids. Blood pressure and oxygenation are still holding; call the rapid response team if systolic pressure drops below 90 or mentation worsens.")
B.asS=new A.eo("Adult four hours after a total hip replacement. An hour ago the blood pressure was 128/80 and heart rate 86. Now slightly restless; the dressing is dry.",104,"112/92",20,96,36.8,B.en,"The pulse pressure has narrowed from 48 to 20 while the heart rate climbs \u2014 an early sign of falling stroke volume, such as concealed bleeding into the thigh. A systolic pressure of 112 still looks acceptable, which is why this is easy to miss. Check the drain, thigh, and urine output and notify the surgeon now; call the rapid response team if systolic pressure falls further.")
B.asY=new A.eo("Adult with heart failure on 2 L oxygen by nasal cannula. Saturation was 96 percent on the same oxygen this morning; there is now a new cough and fine crackles at both lung bases.",98,"146/88",24,91,36.9,B.en,"A saturation falling from 96 to 91 percent on unchanged oxygen, with new crackles and a faster respiratory rate, signals worsening fluid overload before the client is in distress. Raise the head of the bed, check daily weight and intake and output, and notify the provider for treatment such as a diuretic. Turning up the oxygen and charting the number would hide a trend that needs action.")
B.asL=new A.eo("Older adult in long-term care whose temperature normally runs about 36.0 \xb0C. Eating less today and more tired than usual, but oriented.",92,"126/74",20,95,37.8,B.en,"Older adults often have a lower baseline temperature and a blunted fever response, so a rise of 1.1 \xb0C or more above baseline counts as fever. At 37.8 \xb0C this client is 1.8 degrees above normal, and with fatigue and poor intake it may be the first sign of pneumonia or a urinary infection. Do a focused assessment and notify the provider instead of dismissing it as low grade.")
B.asE=new A.eo("Adult admitted after a fall with a head injury. Was talking an hour ago; now opens eyes only to pain, with irregular breathing and long pauses.",50,"188/64",8,93,37.3,B.cW,"Rising systolic pressure with a widening pulse pressure, bradycardia, and irregular respirations is Cushing's triad \u2014 a late sign of increased intracranial pressure and impending brain herniation. Call the rapid response team, support the airway, and keep the head midline with the head of the bed at 30 degrees. The slow heart rate is not reassuring here; it reflects brainstem compression.")
B.asN=new A.eo("Older adult whose metoprolol dose was increased yesterday. Pale, lightheaded when sitting up, and nearly fainted in the bathroom.",38,"82/48",18,95,36.7,B.cW,"A heart rate of 38 with hypotension and near-fainting is symptomatic bradycardia \u2014 cardiac output is failing. Call the rapid response team, keep the client in bed on a cardiac monitor, hold the metoprolol, and prepare atropine 1 mg IV and transcutaneous pacing. Symptoms, not the number alone, make this an emergency; an athlete at 48 with normal pressure is fine.")
B.asP=new A.eo("Adult started on apixaban five days ago for a deep vein thrombosis. Passed two large black, tarry stools this morning; now pale, clammy, and dizzy on standing.",124,"86/52",24,96,36.5,B.cW,"Black, tarry stools mean upper gastrointestinal bleeding, and a new anticoagulant makes it brisk; a heart rate of 124 with a systolic pressure of 86 shows hypovolemic shock is already under way. Call the rapid response team, hold the anticoagulant, place two large-bore IVs, and anticipate a type and crossmatch and a reversal agent. Do not wait for a hemoglobin result to escalate.")
B.asU=new A.eo("Adult six hours after a total thyroidectomy. New high-pitched noise on inspiration, the neck dressing feels tight, and the client is using neck and shoulder muscles to breathe.",126,"152/90",32,87,37,B.cW,"Stridor after thyroid surgery means the upper airway is critically narrowed \u2014 at this stage most often by a hematoma compressing the trachea \u2014 and a saturation of 87 percent with accessory muscle use shows the client is failing to move air. Call the rapid response team and the surgeon, raise the head of the bed, and have the tracheostomy tray at the bedside. Waiting for a provider callback risks complete airway obstruction.")
