B.Zp=s(["15 grams of fast-acting carbohydrate, such as 4 oz of juice","The scheduled dose of insulin","A peanut butter sandwich only","Nothing \u2014 recheck in 4 hours"],t.s)
B.as0=new A.fE("Glucose 54 and the patient can swallow. What do you give?",B.Zp,"The rule of 15: give 15 grams of FAST sugar \u2014 juice, regular soda, or glucose tablets. Insulin would drive the glucose even lower, and protein or fat alone absorbs too slowly to rescue an acute low.",64)
B.Zu=s(["Give another 15 grams of fast-acting carbohydrate","Give a complex-carbohydrate meal now","Give IV dextrose 50%","Document and stop treatment \u2014 64 is close enough"],t.s)
B.arQ=new A.fE("You waited 15 minutes and rechecked: glucose is 64 \u2014 still under 70. Now what?",B.Zu,"Repeat the cycle: 15 more grams of fast carbs, wait 15 minutes, recheck. IV dextrose is reserved for patients who cannot swallow, and treatment does not stop until the glucose is above 70.",86)
B.XE=s(["Give a snack with complex carbohydrate and protein","Give another 15 grams of juice to be safe","Give a correction dose of insulin","No further action is needed"],t.s)
B.arU=new A.fE("The recheck shows 86 \u2014 above 70 at last. What is the final step?",B.XE,"Once the glucose is above 70, a snack with complex carbs and protein \u2014 like crackers with cheese \u2014 keeps it from dropping again, especially if the next meal is more than an hour away. More juice risks a rebound spike; insulin would recreate the emergency.",98)
B.W7=s([B.as0,B.arQ,B.arU],t.q_)
B.arL=new A.oe("Scenario 1: The awake patient","Your patient with type 1 diabetes is sweaty, shaky, and irritable. They are awake and able to swallow. The meter reads 54 mg/dL.",54,B.W7)
B.XX=s(["Give IM glucagon, or IV dextrose 50% if IV access is available","Pour orange juice into their mouth slowly","Place a sugar packet under the tongue and wait","Give a rapid-acting insulin injection"],t.s)
B.arZ=new A.fE("Glucose 38 and the patient is unconscious. What do you do?",B.XX,"Never put anything in an unconscious patient's mouth \u2014 aspiration risk. Glucagon IM (or IV dextrose 50% when a line is available) raises glucose without needing swallowing. Place the patient on their side and recheck in 15 minutes.",95)
B.Xr=s([B.arZ],t.q_)
B.arI=new A.oe("Scenario 2: The unresponsive patient","You find the same patient later \u2014 unresponsive and unable to swallow. The meter reads 38 mg/dL.",38,B.Xr)
B.a_e=s(["Give 15 grams of fast-acting carbohydrate, such as 4 glucose tablets, and recheck in 15 minutes","Give IM glucagon because the client is confused","Give a glass of warm milk and a slice of cheese","Hold the morning glipizide and let the client sleep until breakfast"],t.s)
B.as2=new A.fE("Glucose 58 and the client can swallow. What do you do?",B.a_e,"The client is awake and swallows safely, so the rule of 15 applies: 15 grams of fast sugar, then a recheck in 15 minutes. Glucagon is for a client who cannot take anything by mouth, protein and fat absorb too slowly for an acute low, and holding the next dose does nothing for the glucose that is low now.",84)
B.a0V=s(["Give a snack, then check the glucose frequently per protocol well into the next day","Resume routine before-meal checks \u2014 the glucose is above 70","Wake the client every hour to ask about shakiness or sweating instead of checking glucose","Give 15 grams of juice every hour to prevent another low"],t.s)
B.as1=new A.fE("The recheck is 84 and the client is alert again. What is your plan for the rest of the night?",B.a0V,"Sulfonylureas keep stimulating insulin release for many hours, and older adults clear them slowly, so the low can return long after the first recovery \u2014 keep checking the glucose frequently, often for 24 hours or more. Older adults may not feel warning symptoms, so asking about them is not a safe substitute, and hourly juice causes spikes instead of steady coverage.",96)
B.a_E=s(["Report the overnight low to the provider and ask whether the glipizide dose should be held or changed","Give the glipizide as scheduled \u2014 the glucose is normal now","Give the glipizide with an extra 15 grams of carbohydrate to cover it","Hold the glipizide and document it \u2014 no call is needed because the client recovered"],t.s)
B.arR=new A.fE("At the next check the glucose is 96. The 0800 glipizide dose is coming up. What else does the nurse do?",B.a_E,"The provider needs to know about the low so the sulfonylurea can be reduced or changed \u2014 prolonged lows on these drugs are a known danger in older adults. Giving the usual dose invites another low, and holding it without telling the provider leaves the medication plan unsafe.",102)
B.Wa=s([B.as2,B.as1,B.arR],t.q_)
B.arM=new A.oe("Scenario 3: The older adult on glipizide","At 0300 you find your 78-year-old client, who takes glipizide for type 2 diabetes and ate little at dinner, sweaty and mildly confused. The client is awake, follows commands, and swallows safely. The meter reads 58 mg/dL.",58,B.Wa)
B.a_i=s(["IV dextrose 50% through the existing line, per the hypoglycemia protocol","4 oz of apple juice \u2014 clear liquids are allowed before surgery","IM glucagon, since the client is NPO","Nothing yet \u2014 notify the surgeon first, because surgery may be canceled"],t.s)
B.arW=new A.fE("Glucose 52, drowsy, NPO for surgery, IV in place. What do you give?",B.a_i,"A drowsy client under NPO orders gets nothing by mouth: a lower level of consciousness raises aspiration risk, and oral intake can delay surgery. With an IV already in place, IV dextrose raises the glucose within minutes. Glucagon is for a client with no IV access, and it often causes nausea and vomiting \u2014 a problem before anesthesia.",124)
B.Wh=s(["Keep checking the glucose every 1 to 2 hours, because last night's insulin is still working","Let the client drink 4 oz of juice now that they are alert","Stop monitoring \u2014 the glucose is safely above 70","Give a second dose of dextrose 50% now to prevent another low"],t.s)
B.as_=new A.fE("Fifteen minutes later the client is alert and the glucose is 124. Surgery is 3 hours away. What is the next step?",B.Wh,"IV dextrose 50% works fast but wears off quickly, while the long-acting insulin keeps lowering the glucose for hours \u2014 so recheck every 1 to 2 hours, or per protocol. Being alert does not lift the NPO order, and a second dose of dextrose 50% at 124 is overtreatment.",104)
B.a0K=s(["Notify the provider and anesthesia team of the low so they can adjust the insulin plan and order a dextrose-containing IV","Give the usual morning insulin \u2014 the client is no longer low","Change the IV to dextrose 5% on your own to keep the glucose up","Cancel the surgery, because a low glucose rules it out"],t.s)
B.arO=new A.fE("At the next check the glucose has drifted down to 104. What else does the nurse do before surgery?",B.a0K,"The provider and anesthesia team must know about the low so the insulin plan can be adjusted while the client is NPO, often with a dextrose-containing IV and frequent checks. Giving the usual insulin to a fasting client risks another low, and changing IV fluids or canceling surgery is not the nurse's independent decision.",112)
B.Ze=s([B.arW,B.as_,B.arO],t.q_)
B.arJ=new A.oe("Scenario 4: The NPO client before surgery","Your client with type 2 diabetes is NPO for surgery this morning and has an IV of 0.9% sodium chloride running. They took their full dose of long-acting insulin last night. Now they are drowsy and clammy, and the meter reads 52 mg/dL.",52,B.Ze)
B.ZC=s(["15 grams of fast-acting carbohydrate, such as 4 oz of juice, as the child's diabetes plan directs","A chocolate bar from the child's backpack","Glucagon from the child's emergency kit","Nothing yet \u2014 have the child rest and recheck in 15 minutes"],t.s)
B.arP=new A.fE("Glucose 62 and the child can swallow. What do you give?",B.ZC,"An alert child who can swallow gets fast sugar by mouth \u2014 about 15 grams for a school-age child, or the amount in the diabetes medical management plan. Chocolate's fat slows absorption, glucagon is only for a child who cannot swallow or is unresponsive, and resting without treatment lets the glucose keep falling.",81)
B.Wk=s(["Give a snack with complex carbohydrate and protein, such as crackers and cheese","Give another 15 grams of juice to keep the glucose climbing","Send the child back to class \u2014 the glucose is above 70","Send the child to the cafeteria to pick up a snack"],t.s)
B.arS=new A.fE("Fifteen minutes later the child feels better and the meter reads 81. Lunch is more than an hour away. What next?",B.Wk,"With the glucose above 70 and lunch more than an hour away, a snack with complex carbohydrate and protein keeps it from falling again. More juice only causes a spike and another drop, and a child who was just low should never be sent anywhere alone \u2014 the low can return on the way.",94)
B.a12=s(["Have an adult walk the child back to class, and call the parent about the low and the treatment given","Let the child walk back to class alone \u2014 they feel fine now","Send the child home on the school bus to rest for the day","Document the low only \u2014 the parent will see it in the health log"],t.s)
B.arY=new A.fE("The child has eaten the snack and the recheck reads 94. What does the nurse do before the child leaves your office?",B.a12,"An adult escorts a child who is recovering from a low, and the parent is told about every low and its treatment so patterns and insulin doses can be reviewed. Walking alone or riding the bus home leaves the child unsupervised if the glucose drops again, and a log entry alone does not inform the parent in time.",98)
B.a0X=s([B.arP,B.arS,B.arY],t.q_)
B.arK=new A.oe("Scenario 5: The child at school","You are the school nurse. At 10:30 a teacher walks an 8-year-old with type 1 diabetes into your office. The child is shaky, pale, and sweaty but alert and able to swallow. Lunch is not until 12:15. The meter reads 62 mg/dL.",62,B.a0X)
B.ZH=s(["IM glucagon per the protocol, while a new IV is started","Small sips of juice with the head of the bed raised to 90 degrees","Glucose gel squeezed inside the cheek so it absorbs without swallowing","Nothing until a new IV is in for dextrose 50%"],t.s)
B.arV=new A.fE("Glucose 48. The client is awake but cannot swallow safely and has no IV. What do you give?",B.ZH,"With no safe swallow and no IV, IM glucagon raises the glucose in about 10 to 15 minutes without anything by mouth. Thin liquids are what a client with dysphagia aspirates, even sitting upright, and gel in the cheek is mostly swallowed rather than absorbed. Start an IV, but do not delay treatment waiting for one.",86)
B.Yc=s(["Turn the client onto their side with the head of the bed raised, and keep suction at the bedside","Lay the client flat on their back to ease the nausea","Give an oral antiemetic tablet with a small sip of water","Place the client in Trendelenburg to improve blood flow to the brain"],t.s)
B.arT=new A.fE("Fifteen minutes later the glucose is 86, but the client is nauseated and gagging \u2014 a common glucagon side effect. How do you protect the airway?",B.Yc,"Glucagon often causes vomiting, and a client with dysphagia cannot protect their airway well. Lying on the side with the head raised lets vomit drain from the mouth, with suction ready. Flat or head-down positions make aspiration more likely, and nothing \u2014 not even a pill \u2014 goes by mouth.",92)
B.a0Q=s(["Ask the provider for an ongoing glucose source, such as a dextrose infusion, and keep checking the glucose closely","Give crackers and cheese so the glucose does not drop again","Stop checking \u2014 92 is safely above 70","Give dextrose 50% through the new IV to push the glucose higher"],t.s)
B.arX=new A.fE("A new IV is in and the recheck reads 92. The client still cannot eat. What is the next step?",B.a0Q,"Glucagon's effect is short-lived and the client cannot eat, so the low will return without an ongoing glucose source such as a dextrose-containing IV \u2014 and the glucose needs close rechecks. The usual follow-up snack is unsafe with dysphagia, and dextrose 50% treats a low, not a glucose of 92.",110)
B.a03=s([B.arV,B.arT,B.arX],t.q_)
B.arN=new A.oe("Scenario 6: The client who cannot swallow safely","Your client had a stroke 2 days ago and has dysphagia, so they are NPO until a swallow study. Their IV infiltrated and was removed an hour ago. They are awake but confused, pale, and sweaty, and the meter reads 48 mg/dL. A hypoglycemia protocol is ordered.",48,B.a03)
