B.apK=new A.hY("dopamine",B.dZ,"Ca\xb2\u207a\nchannel","D2 dopamine receptors","drug",0.78,0.5,B.kt,!1,1,!1,!1,"Dopamine cannot dock \u2192 hallucinations and delusions fade","Dopamine is still released, but the drug caps the D2 receptors so the message is not received \u2014 too much blockade shows up as movement side effects.",".","Dopamine still crosses the gap but has nowhere to bind.","It blocks them. Positive symptoms of psychosis ease; blocking dopamine in movement pathways causes EPS (tremor, rigidity, dystonia).","At D2 receptors. Watch for EPS, tardive dyskinesia, and neuroleptic malignant syndrome (fever, rigidity, autonomic instability).")
B.apL=new A.hY("GABA (the brain's brake)",B.aD,"Cl\u207b\nchannel","GABA-A receptors","drug",0.78,0.5,null,!0,1,!1,!1,"GABA works harder \u2192 calm, sleepy, muscles relax","The drug sits beside GABA on its receptor and makes GABA's calming effect stronger \u2014 more chloride enters and the neuron quiets.",".","The same amount of GABA crosses \u2014 the drug does not add GABA, it makes each GABA molecule more effective at the receptor.","It boosts GABA's effect: chloride floods in, the neuron is harder to excite \u2192 sedation, anxiolysis, seizure control.","At the GABA-A receptor. Watch respirations and sedation, avoid alcohol/opioids, fall precautions; flumazenil reverses overdose.")
B.Aa=new A.c(0.27,0.22)
B.apM=new A.hY("excitatory glutamate",B.dZ,"Na\u207a\nchannel","Glutamate receptors","drug",0.27,0.38,B.Aa,!1,0.4,!1,!1,"Neuron cannot fire rapid-fire \u2192 seizure spread stops","Blocking sodium channels stops the neuron from firing in rapid bursts, so a seizure cannot spread from cell to cell."," \u2014 firing depends on sodium rushing in, which this drug slows.","Less transmitter is released because the neuron cannot depolarize in rapid succession.","Nothing directly; downstream neurons just receive fewer bursts.","On the sodium channel. Narrow therapeutic range \u2014 check drug levels, watch for nystagmus, ataxia and gum overgrowth; never stop abruptly.")
B.apN=new A.hY("excitatory glutamate",B.dZ,"Ca\xb2\u207a\nchannel","Glutamate receptors","drug",0.27,0.38,B.Aa,!1,0.4,!1,!1,"Less excitatory firing \u2192 fewer seizures, less nerve pain","The drug binds the calcium channel on the sending neuron, so fewer vesicles release and the over-excited signal calms down."," \u2014 but calcium must enter first, and that is what this drug limits.","Less transmitter crosses the gap because calcium entry into the terminal is reduced \u2014 the signal is turned down, not off.","Nothing directly \u2014 the receptors simply receive less transmitter. That is why the effect is calming rather than sedating.","On the presynaptic calcium channel. Watch for drowsiness, dizziness and ataxia; taper rather than stop abruptly.")
B.apO=new A.hY("multiple transmitters",B.x,"Ca\xb2\u207a\nchannel","Postsynaptic signalling","Li\u207a",0.5,0.86,null,!1,1,!1,!1,"Signalling evened out \u2192 mania and depression both blunted","Lithium dampens the second-messenger systems inside neurons so the swings of mania are flattened \u2014 a narrow window between working and toxic.",".","Release is roughly normal; the change happens inside the receiving neuron's signalling machinery.","It quiets over-active signalling downstream of the receptors.","Inside the neuron's signalling cascade. Therapeutic level 0.6-1.2; toxicity above 1.5 (tremor, vomiting, confusion). Dehydration and low sodium raise levels.")
B.a4e=new A.c(0.36,0.72)
B.apP=new A.hY("serotonin",B.bg,"Ca\xb2\u207a\nchannel","Serotonin receptors","SSRI",0.45,0.86,B.a4e,!1,2,!0,!1,"More serotonin stays in the gap \u2192 mood lifts (over weeks)","The reuptake pump normally vacuums serotonin back up. Block the pump and serotonin lingers in the gap, strengthening the signal.",".","It stays longer: the pump that recycles it is blocked, so more serotonin is available to the receptors.","Receptors see more serotonin for longer. Full benefit takes 4-6 weeks as the receptors adapt.","On the reuptake pump. Watch for serotonin syndrome (agitation, fever, tremor) especially with other serotonergic drugs, and for suicidal thoughts early in treatment.")
B.apQ=new A.hY("pain signal",B.dZ,"Ca\xb2\u207a\nchannel","Mu opioid receptors","naloxone",0.78,0.5,B.kt,!1,1,!1,!1,"Opioid knocked off \u2192 breathing returns (and pain returns)","Naloxone fits the mu receptor better than the opioid and pushes it off, reversing respiratory depression within minutes.",".","Pain signalling resumes as soon as the opioid is displaced.","It occupies and blocks them \u2014 no activation, so the opioid effects stop abruptly.","At mu receptors, displacing the opioid. It wears off before many opioids do \u2014 keep monitoring for re-sedation and repeat doses.")
B.apR=new A.hY("serotonin",B.bg,"Ca\xb2\u207a\nchannel","5-HT3 receptors (vomiting center)","drug",0.78,0.5,B.kt,!1,1,!1,!1,"Nausea signal never arrives","Chemotherapy and anesthesia trigger serotonin release that fires the vomiting center. The drug caps the 5-HT3 receptors so the signal dies.",".","Serotonin is still released but cannot bind.","Blocked \u2014 the vomiting reflex is not triggered.","At 5-HT3 receptors in the gut and brainstem. Give before the trigger; watch for headache, constipation and QT prolongation.")
B.a4q=new A.c(0.5,0.2)
B.apS=new A.hY("acetylcholine",B.x,"Ca\xb2\u207a\nchannel","Acetylcholine receptors","drug",0.5,0.33,B.a4q,!1,2,!1,!0,"Acetylcholine survives longer \u2192 memory signalling supported","An enzyme in the gap normally chops up acetylcholine. Blocking the enzyme lets acetylcholine linger, supporting memory pathways.",".","It is broken down more slowly because the enzyme is inhibited, so more of it reaches the receptors.","They receive acetylcholine for longer.","On the breakdown enzyme (acetylcholinesterase). Expect GI upset and watch for bradycardia \u2014 cholinergic effects.")
B.apT=new A.hY("dopamine",B.dZ,"Ca\xb2\u207a\nchannel","Dopamine receptors","L-dopa",0.12,0.2,null,!0,2,!1,!1,"Refilled dopamine \u2192 smoother movement, less rigidity","In Parkinson's the dopamine neurons are dying. Levodopa crosses into the brain and is converted to dopamine, refilling the vesicles.",".","More dopamine crosses because the terminal has been restocked.","They receive dopamine again, so movement improves \u2014 but effect can wear off between doses over time.","It is converted to dopamine inside the neuron. Give on a schedule, avoid high-protein meals with doses, watch for orthostatic hypotension and dyskinesias.")
B.apU=new A.hY("acetylcholine",B.x,"Ca\xb2\u207a\nchannel","Muscarinic receptors","drug",0.78,0.5,B.kt,!1,1,!1,!1,"ACh blocked \u2192 can't see, pee, spit or poop",'Acetylcholine runs "rest and digest" through muscarinic receptors. The drug caps them: saliva and sweat dry up, the bladder and bowel slow, vision blurs and the heart speeds up. Benztropine uses the same block to ease antipsychotic tremor and rigidity.',".","Acetylcholine is still released and crosses the gap, but the receptors are capped, so the rest-and-digest message is lost.","It blocks them (an antagonist). Dry mouth, urinary retention, constipation, blurred vision, tachycardia and heat intolerance from less sweating \u2014 and confusion or delirium, especially in older adults.","At muscarinic receptors. Benztropine treats antipsychotic EPS; a scopolamine patch prevents motion sickness. Teach sugar-free gum or sips of water, fiber and fluids, voiding checks and avoiding overheating; watch older adults for confusion.")
B.apV=new A.hY("pain signal",B.dZ,"Ca\xb2\u207a\nchannel","Mu opioid receptors","opioid",0.78,0.5,null,!0,0.4,!1,!1,"Pain signal muted \u2014 but breathing drive muted too","The drug activates mu receptors, which stop pain signals from passing on. The same receptors in the brainstem slow breathing.",".","Pain transmitters are released less, and the receiving neuron is quieter, so the pain message fades.","It activates them (an agonist). Analgesia, euphoria, sedation, and slowed respirations all come from the same action.","At mu receptors. Count respirations before each dose (hold if below 12), monitor sedation, expect constipation; naloxone reverses.")
